SLIX musl POSIX shim
====================

Arch-neutral implementation of the musl POSIX surface that runs in
kernel mode (EL1 on aarch64, ring 0 on x86_64) inside the syscall
handler. Each architecture's syscall entry decodes the syscall
number from the saved frame and tail-calls `posix_dispatch` here
with up to six i64 args; the dispatcher routes by the SLIX-local
syscall number (>= 128) to the right `sys_*` handler.

Handlers do their own work in two ways:

- **Local ops** (POSIX fd table bookkeeping, STDIO writes,
  per-process state) execute directly in this layer.
- **Server-backed ops** (sockets via inet, files / pipes / TTY via
  VFS) translate to IPC messages on the matching server port.

The shim runs on the user process's page table (the syscall does
not switch PTBR), so user pointers dereference directly. For VFS
calls that need to copy across address spaces, the shim creates a
kernel-side memory grant via `kernel_grant_create_inline` and
hands the grant id to VFS, which `grant_map`s the page into its
own address space.

Architecture interface — only one function is arch-specific:

- `extern shim_uart_putc(c: int)` — write a byte directly to the
  platform UART. Used for the STDOUT / STDERR fast path until TTY
  routing is wired in. Each arch provides this as a no-mangle
  global symbol from its assembly file (PL011 MMIO on aarch64,
  COM1 port-I/O on x86_64).

Everything else this file uses comes from the shared microkernel
modules (`oskit.ipc`, `oskit.services`, `oskit.kernel`).

    module oskit.posix

    import oskit.ipc.{port_lookup, ipc_send}
    import oskit.services.{sleep, notify_wait, notify_read_self, sleep_or_notify, uptime}
    import oskit.kernel.{current_thread, terminate_current,
        kernel_grant_create_inline, kernel_grant_revoke_inline,
        kernel_get_thread_pid, kernel_get_process_main_tid}

    extern shim_uart_putc(c: int)


POSIX fd table
==============

Per-process POSIX file descriptor table. Each process gets 64
slots; slots 0/1/2 are lazily initialized to STDIN/STDOUT/STDERR
so a fresh musl process can `write(1, ...)` without any explicit
setup from PM.

Slot kinds:

- `POSIX_FD_FREE` — slot is available for allocation.
- `POSIX_FD_STDIN` — read returns EOF, write is rejected.
- `POSIX_FD_STDOUT` / `POSIX_FD_STDERR` — write goes directly to
  the UART (retained fast-path until TTY routing lands), read is
  rejected.
- `POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET` — `target` holds the inet server's
  socket id; read/write/close/sendto/recvfrom route through
  `ipc_send` to the inet port.
- `POSIX_FD_FILE` — `target` holds a VFS handle (file, TTY,
  pipe end, or TCP via VFS scheme); all read/write/close/lseek
  forward to the VFS port. `pos` is unused (VFS tracks
  position server-side). `flags` carries POSIX flags
  (O_NONBLOCK, etc.) the shim consults locally.

Generation tracking uses `processes[pid].main_tid` as the sentinel:
when a pid slot is reused by a new process, the new `main_tid`
differs from the last-initialized value, which triggers a fresh
table reset. That avoids leaking fd state across process reuse
without a PM-side cleanup hook.

Not handled in this pass: process-exit cleanup of held socket
handles on the inet server side (the inet record still quotes the
terminated thread as owner). Future work tracked under the
posix_fd_bridge memory.

    val POSIX_FD_FREE        = 0
    val POSIX_FD_STDIN       = 1
    val POSIX_FD_STDOUT      = 2
    val POSIX_FD_STDERR      = 3
    val POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET  = 4
    val POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET  = 5
    val POSIX_FD_FILE        = 6
    val POSIX_FD_EPOLL       = 7
    val POSIX_FD_EVENTFD     = 8
    val POSIX_FD_TIMERFD     = 9

    val POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC   = 64

    struct PosixFd
        kind: int
        target: int
        flags: int
        pos: i64
        // setsockopt(SO_SNDBUF/SO_RCVBUF) requested sizes that
        // arrived before the inet slot existed.  0 = "no override,
        // use the inet-side default".  Applied via INET_CMD_TCP_SETBUF
        // immediately after connect/listen establishes the slot.
        pending_sndbuf: int
        pending_rcvbuf: int
        // Persisted setsockopt flag values so getsockopt echoes the
        // actually-requested value instead of zero (Linux-compat for
        // option introspection).  These flags are not yet enforced
        // — REUSEADDR doesn't relax bind, KEEPALIVE has no idle
        // timer, NODELAY would only matter if Nagle existed.  Real
        // enforcement is its own follow-up.
        flag_reuseaddr: int
        flag_broadcast: int
        flag_keepalive: int
        flag_reuseport: int
        flag_nodelay: int
        // SO_LINGER state. `linger_on != 0` activates the option;
        // `linger_secs == 0` (with linger_on) is the "abort-on-close
        // → RST instead of FIN" shortcut, the only enforcement
        // landed so far. Non-zero linger_secs is persisted but its
        // flush-or-timeout semantics aren't enforced yet.
        linger_on: int
        linger_secs: int
        // setsockopt(IPPROTO_TCP, TCP_KEEPIDLE/INTVL/CNT) — caller-
        // requested per-socket keepalive overrides. 0 = "no override,
        // use the inet-side default".  Idle/intvl persisted as the
        // user's seconds value (the conversion to ticks happens at
        // the IPC boundary so getsockopt round-trips the seconds
        // unchanged); cnt is a raw probe count.
        keep_idle_secs: int
        keep_intvl_secs: int
        keep_cnt: int
        // POSIX_FD_EVENTFD state. `eventfd_count` is the running
        // counter; read returns the count and resets it (or
        // decrements to 0 in semaphore mode), write adds to it.
        // `eventfd_semaphore` records EFD_SEMAPHORE so successive
        // reads decrement by 1 instead of returning the full
        // counter at once.
        eventfd_count: i64
        eventfd_semaphore: int
        // POSIX_FD_TIMERFD state. `interval_ticks` is the periodic
        // re-arm in scheduler ticks (100 Hz); 0 means one-shot.
        // `next_tick` is the absolute uptime() tick at which the
        // next expiration is due; 0 means disarmed. `expirations`
        // accumulates fires that haven't been consumed via read()
        // yet; the next read returns the count and resets it.
        timerfd_interval_ticks: int
        timerfd_next_tick: int
        timerfd_expirations: i64

    var posix_fd_table: [1024]PosixFd     // 16 procs * 64 fds
    var posix_fd_last_gen: [16]int        // main_tid when last initialized

    posix_fd_init_pid(pid: int)
        val base = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC
        var i = 0
        while i < POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC
            posix_fd_table[base + i].kind = POSIX_FD_FREE
            posix_fd_table[base + i].target = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].flags = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].pos = i64(0)
            posix_fd_table[base + i].pending_sndbuf = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].pending_rcvbuf = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].flag_reuseaddr = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].flag_broadcast = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].flag_keepalive = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].flag_reuseport = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].flag_nodelay = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].linger_on = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].linger_secs = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].keep_idle_secs = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].keep_intvl_secs = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].keep_cnt = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].eventfd_count = i64(0)
            posix_fd_table[base + i].eventfd_semaphore = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].timerfd_interval_ticks = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].timerfd_next_tick = 0
            posix_fd_table[base + i].timerfd_expirations = i64(0)
            i += 1
        posix_fd_table[base + 0].kind = POSIX_FD_STDIN
        posix_fd_table[base + 1].kind = POSIX_FD_STDOUT
        posix_fd_table[base + 2].kind = POSIX_FD_STDERR

    posix_fd_ensure_init(pid: int)
        val gen = kernel_get_process_main_tid(pid)
        if posix_fd_last_gen[pid] != gen
            posix_fd_init_pid(pid)
            posix_fd_last_gen[pid] = gen

    posix_fd_current_pid() -> int
        kernel_get_thread_pid(current_thread)

    posix_fd_kind(fd: int) -> int
        if fd < 0
            return POSIX_FD_FREE
        if fd >= POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC
            return POSIX_FD_FREE
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        if pid <= 0
            return POSIX_FD_FREE
        if pid >= 16
            return POSIX_FD_FREE
        posix_fd_ensure_init(pid)
        posix_fd_table[pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd].kind

    posix_fd_target(fd: int) -> int
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        posix_fd_table[pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd].target

    posix_fd_flags(fd: int) -> int
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        posix_fd_table[pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd].flags

    posix_fd_set_flags(fd: int, flags: int)
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        posix_fd_table[pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd].flags = flags

    posix_fd_alloc(kind: int, target: int) -> int
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        if pid <= 0
            return -1
        if pid >= 16
            return -1
        posix_fd_ensure_init(pid)
        val base = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC
        var i = 3
        while i < POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC
            if posix_fd_table[base + i].kind == POSIX_FD_FREE
                posix_fd_table[base + i].kind = kind
                posix_fd_table[base + i].target = target
                posix_fd_table[base + i].flags = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].pos = i64(0)
                posix_fd_table[base + i].pending_sndbuf = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].pending_rcvbuf = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].flag_reuseaddr = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].flag_broadcast = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].flag_keepalive = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].flag_reuseport = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].flag_nodelay = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].linger_on = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].linger_secs = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].keep_idle_secs = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].keep_intvl_secs = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].keep_cnt = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].eventfd_count = i64(0)
                posix_fd_table[base + i].eventfd_semaphore = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].timerfd_interval_ticks = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].timerfd_next_tick = 0
                posix_fd_table[base + i].timerfd_expirations = i64(0)
                return i
            i += 1
        -1

Iteration-based "is there another fd in this process pointing
at the same backend?" check. Used by `sys_close` to avoid issuing
an inet close while a `dup`-ped fd still references the same
inet record. STDIO kinds share kind/target = (1/2/3, 0), so dup
of STDOUT works the same way: don't tear down anything when one
of two pointing at the same target closes.

    posix_fd_other_dup_exists(fd: int, kind: int, target: int) -> int
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        if pid <= 0
            return 0
        if pid >= 16
            return 0
        val base = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC
        var i = 0
        while i < POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC
            if i != fd
                if posix_fd_table[base + i].kind == kind
                    if posix_fd_table[base + i].target == target
                        return 1
            i += 1
        0

    posix_fd_clear(fd: int)
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        if pid <= 0
            return
        if pid >= 16
            return
        if fd < 0
            return
        if fd >= POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC
            return
        val idx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
        posix_fd_table[idx].kind = POSIX_FD_FREE
        posix_fd_table[idx].target = 0
        posix_fd_table[idx].flags = 0
        posix_fd_table[idx].pos = i64(0)


INET client helpers (shim-side)
-------------------------------

Minimal wire helpers that mirror `oskit/servers/inet.lsysl`'s
message layout. The shim talks to the inet server via the same
IPC protocol that `oskit/net/client.lsysl` uses from user space;
we replicate the writers here instead of importing `oskit.net` to
keep the kernel link free of the full socket-API module.

    val INET_CMD_SOCKET      = 1
    val INET_CMD_BIND        = 2
    val INET_CMD_SENDTO      = 3
    val INET_CMD_RECVFROM    = 4
    val INET_CMD_CLOSE       = 5
    val INET_CMD_TCP_CONNECT = 10
    val INET_CMD_TCP_SEND    = 11
    val INET_CMD_TCP_RECV    = 12
    val INET_CMD_TCP_CLOSE   = 13
    val INET_CMD_TCP_LISTEN  = 14
    val INET_CMD_TCP_ACCEPT  = 15
    val INET_CMD_UDP_GETNAME = 24
    val INET_CMD_TCP_GETNAME = 25
    val INET_CMD_RECVFROM_TIMEOUT = 18
    val INET_CMD_TCP_RECV_NB = 26
    val INET_CMD_TCP_SHUTDOWN = 27
    val INET_CMD_POLL        = 28
    val INET_CMD_EPOLL_SUB   = 29
    val INET_CMD_EPOLL_UNSUB = 30
    val INET_CMD_EPOLL_INST_CLOSE = 31
    val INET_CMD_TCP_ACCEPT_NB = 32
    val INET_CMD_TCP_CONNECT_NB = 33
    val INET_CMD_TCP_SETBUF  = 34
    val INET_CMD_TCP_GETBUF  = 35
    val INET_CMD_TCP_ABORT_CLOSE = 36
    val INET_CMD_TCP_SET_KEEPALIVE = 37
    val INET_CMD_TCP_SET_KEEPOPT = 39
    val INET_CMD_TCP_GET_KEEPOPT = 40

    val INET_MAX_TCP_SEG     = 480

    val POSIX_AF_INET        = 2
    val POSIX_SOCK_DGRAM     = 2
    val POSIX_SOCK_STREAM    = 1

POSIX `O_NONBLOCK` is Linux octal 04000 = 2048. We only honour this
flag today; other open(2) flags (O_APPEND etc.) would need the
shim to actually track them, so we mask them off on `F_SETFL`.

    val POSIX_O_NONBLOCK     = 2048

fcntl command codes (`<fcntl.h>` in musl arch).

    val POSIX_F_DUPFD        = 0
    val POSIX_F_GETFD        = 1
    val POSIX_F_SETFD        = 2
    val POSIX_F_GETFL        = 3
    val POSIX_F_SETFL        = 4

    var inet_port_cached = -1

    inet_get_port() -> int
        if inet_port_cached < 0
            inet_port_cached = port_lookup("inet")
        inet_port_cached

    shim_write_u16(p: *byte, off: int, v: int)
        p[off]     = byte((v >> 8) & 0xff)
        p[off + 1] = byte(v & 0xff)

    shim_read_u16(p: *byte, off: int) -> int
        ((int(p[off]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(p[off + 1]) & 0xff)


VFS client helpers (shim-side)
------------------------------

The shim talks to VFS the same way `oskit/fs/client.lsysl` does:
big-endian wire format, command numbers from `oskit/servers
/vfs.lsysl`. We replicate the writers locally to avoid pulling
the user-space FS module into the kernel link.

VFS read/write protocol uses memory grants — the shim creates a
grant against the *user* buffer (granter_pid = current pid) and
hands the grant id to VFS, which `grant_map`s the page into its
own address space, copies, and returns. The grant is revoked on
the way out so it doesn't leak.

    val VFS_CMD_OPEN_C    = 1
    val VFS_CMD_READ_C    = 2
    val VFS_CMD_WRITE_C   = 3
    val VFS_CMD_CLOSE_C   = 13
    val VFS_CMD_SEEK_C    = 14
    val VFS_CMD_CONNECT_C = 15
    val VFS_CMD_PIPE_C    = 18
    // Phase A2 epoll bridge — mirrors INET_CMD_EPOLL_*.
    val VFS_CMD_POLL_C              = 20
    val VFS_CMD_EPOLL_SUB_C         = 21
    val VFS_CMD_EPOLL_UNSUB_C       = 22
    val VFS_CMD_EPOLL_INST_CLOSE_C  = 23

`GRANT_READ` / `GRANT_WRITE` mirror the values in `oskit.kernel`'s
private grant table (1 = read by mapper, 2 = write by mapper).

    val SHIM_GRANT_READ  = 1
    val SHIM_GRANT_WRITE = 2

    var vfs_port_cached = -1

    vfs_get_port() -> int
        if vfs_port_cached < 0
            vfs_port_cached = port_lookup("fs")
        vfs_port_cached


    var tty_port_cached = -1

    tty_get_port() -> int
        if tty_port_cached < 0
            tty_port_cached = port_lookup("tty")
        tty_port_cached


TTY IPC commands (must track oskit/drivers/tty/tty.lsysl).
The shim only uses READ + POLL + EPOLL_SUB/UNSUB/INST_CLOSE;
the WRITE/PUTS/SWITCH commands are driven from the legacy ulib.

    val TTY_CMD_READ_C            = 2
    val TTY_CMD_POLL_C            = 5
    val TTY_CMD_EPOLL_SUB_C       = 6
    val TTY_CMD_EPOLL_UNSUB_C     = 7
    val TTY_CMD_EPOLL_INST_CLOSE_C = 8

Big-endian 32-bit helpers for the VFS wire format.

    shim_write_be_i32(p: *byte, off: int, v: int)
        p[off]     = byte((v >> 24) & 0xff)
        p[off + 1] = byte((v >> 16) & 0xff)
        p[off + 2] = byte((v >> 8) & 0xff)
        p[off + 3] = byte(v & 0xff)

    shim_read_be_i32(p: *byte, off: int) -> int
        ((int(p[off]) & 0xff) << 24) | ((int(p[off + 1]) & 0xff) << 16) | ((int(p[off + 2]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(p[off + 3]) & 0xff)


epoll instance table (shim-side)
--------------------------------

Per-process level-triggered epoll. Each `POSIX_FD_EPOLL` slot
points at an instance row here; the row holds a small array of
watched (fd, events_mask, user_data) tuples. `events_mask` is
the user's request bits (`EPOLLIN`/`EPOLLOUT`/...); `user_data`
is the 8-byte cookie from `struct epoll_event.data` echoed
back unchanged on every wake.

`epoll_pwait` polls each watched fd's readiness via
`INET_CMD_POLL` (sockets) or by treating files/pipes as
always-ready (a stopgap until VFS grows a polling primitive).
Phase A2 layered an inet→shim notify path on top so indefinite
waits block via `notify_wait()` instead of polling.

The table is sized for 4 instances per process, 32 watched
fds each — way more than the smoke tests need but cheap.
Generation tracking via `posix_fd_last_gen` reuses the same
sentinel as `posix_fd_table` so a stale instance from a
previous tenant of the pid is reset on first touch.

    val POSIX_EPOLL_INSTANCES_PER_PROC = 4
    val POSIX_EPOLL_ENTRIES_PER_INSTANCE = 32
    val POSIX_EPOLL_TABLE_SIZE = 64    // 16 procs * 4 instances

EPOLL_* event bits (must match musl's `<sys/epoll.h>`).

    val POSIX_EPOLLIN       = 0x001
    val POSIX_EPOLLOUT      = 0x004
    val POSIX_EPOLLERR      = 0x008
    val POSIX_EPOLLHUP      = 0x010
    val POSIX_EPOLLONESHOT  = 0x40000000
    val POSIX_EPOLLET       = -2147483648    // 1 << 31, signed view

EPOLL_CTL_* opcodes.

    val POSIX_EPOLL_CTL_ADD = 1
    val POSIX_EPOLL_CTL_DEL = 2
    val POSIX_EPOLL_CTL_MOD = 3

`struct epoll_event` on LP64 little-endian (matches both aarch64
and x86_64 musl layouts): events @0 (4), pad @4 (4), data @8
(8) = 16 bytes total. We store events as int and data as i64;
the shim copies bytes 0..3 and 8..15 of the user struct.

    val POSIX_EPOLL_EVENT_SIZE = 16

    struct EpollEntry
        in_use: int
        watched_fd: int
        events: int
        data_lo: i64
        last_reported: int   // for EPOLLET: bits last delivered
        armed: int           // 0 if EPOLLONESHOT fired and disarmed
        // Cached per-fd fire counter (returned by INET_CMD_POLL /
        // VFS_CMD_POLL). When the live counter advances past this,
        // the fd actually crossed an edge — we clear `last_reported`
        // so EPOLLET re-fires. Initialised to 0 on EPOLL_CTL_ADD,
        // -1 sentinel could've worked but 0 lines up with the
        // server-side counter starting at 0 for fresh slots.
        last_seen_seq: int

    struct EpollInstance
        in_use: int
        owner_gen: int     // matches kernel_get_process_main_tid
        entries: [32]EpollEntry

    var epoll_table: [64]EpollInstance

    epoll_inst_init(idx: int)
        epoll_table[idx].in_use = 0
        epoll_table[idx].owner_gen = 0
        var i = 0
        while i < POSIX_EPOLL_ENTRIES_PER_INSTANCE
            epoll_table[idx].entries[i].in_use = 0
            epoll_table[idx].entries[i].watched_fd = 0
            epoll_table[idx].entries[i].events = 0
            epoll_table[idx].entries[i].data_lo = i64(0)
            epoll_table[idx].entries[i].last_reported = 0
            epoll_table[idx].entries[i].armed = 0
            epoll_table[idx].entries[i].last_seen_seq = 0
            i += 1

    epoll_alloc_inst() -> int
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        if pid <= 0
            return -1
        if pid >= 16
            return -1
        val gen = kernel_get_process_main_tid(pid)
        val base = pid * POSIX_EPOLL_INSTANCES_PER_PROC
        var i = 0
        while i < POSIX_EPOLL_INSTANCES_PER_PROC
            if epoll_table[base + i].in_use == 0
                epoll_inst_init(base + i)
                epoll_table[base + i].in_use = 1
                epoll_table[base + i].owner_gen = gen
                return base + i
            // Stale instance from a dead tenant — reclaim it.
            if epoll_table[base + i].owner_gen != gen
                epoll_inst_init(base + i)
                epoll_table[base + i].in_use = 1
                epoll_table[base + i].owner_gen = gen
                return base + i
            i += 1
        -1

    epoll_inst_release(idx: int)
        if idx < 0
            return
        if idx >= POSIX_EPOLL_TABLE_SIZE
            return
        epoll_inst_init(idx)


POSIX shim dispatch. SLIX-local syscall numbers start at 128; the
musl arch headers (`slix/musl/arch/<arch>-slix/bits/syscall.h.in`)
define the same numbers cross-arch. Handlers take the raw user-
space args and return bytes-written / 0-on-success / -errno as
i64, which the arch's syscall entry writes into the saved arg-0
register.

All fd-carrying syscalls route through the per-process fd table
above; see `posix_fd_kind` / `posix_fd_target`. Socket syscalls
translate to inet-server IPC, giving musl programs direct access
to the same UDP stack that native sysl programs use via
`oskit.net.client`.

    posix_dispatch(num: int, a0: i64, a1: i64, a2: i64, a3: i64, a4: i64, a5: i64) -> i64
        if num == 128
            return sys_write(int(a0), a1, int(a2))
        if num == 129
            terminate_current(int(a0))
            return i64(0)
        if num == 130
            return sys_read(int(a0), a1, int(a2))
        if num == 131
            return sys_accept(int(a0), a1, a2)
        if num == 132
            return sys_accept4(int(a0), a1, a2, int(a3))
        if num == 135
            return sys_bind(int(a0), a1, int(a2))
        if num == 147
            return sys_close(int(a0))
        if num == 148
            return sys_connect(int(a0), a1, int(a2))
        if num == 151
            return sys_dup(int(a0))
        if num == 152
            return sys_dup3(int(a0), int(a1), int(a2))
        if num == 217
            return sys_listen(int(a0), int(a1))
        if num == 276
            return sys_recvfrom(int(a0), a1, int(a2), int(a3), a4, a5)
        if num == 278
            return sys_recvmsg(int(a0), a1, int(a2))
        if num == 306
            return sys_sendmsg(int(a0), a1, int(a2))
        if num == 307
            return sys_sendto(int(a0), a1, int(a2), int(a3), a4, int(a5))
        if num == 171
            return sys_fcntl(int(a0), int(a1), int(a2))
        if num == 192
            return sys_getpeername(int(a0), a1, a2)
        if num == 203
            return sys_getsockname(int(a0), a1, a2)
        if num == 204
            return sys_getsockopt(int(a0), int(a1), int(a2), a3, a4)
        if num == 326
            return sys_setsockopt(int(a0), int(a1), int(a2), a3, int(a4))
        if num == 334
            return sys_shutdown(int(a0), int(a1))
        if num == 337
            return sys_socket(int(a0), int(a1), int(a2))
        if num == 254
            return sys_openat(int(a0), a1, int(a2), int(a3))
        if num == 221
            return sys_lseek(int(a0), a1, int(a2))
        if num == 256
            return sys_pipe2(a0, int(a1))
        if num == 153
            return sys_epoll_create1(int(a0))
        if num == 154
            return sys_epoll_ctl(int(a0), int(a1), int(a2), a3)
        if num == 155
            return sys_epoll_pwait(int(a0), a1, int(a2), int(a3))
        if num == 156
            return sys_eventfd2(int(a0), int(a1))
        if num == 356
            return sys_timerfd_create(int(a0), int(a1))
        if num == 357
            return sys_timerfd_gettime(int(a0), a1)
        if num == 358
            return sys_timerfd_settime(int(a0), int(a1), a2, a3)
        i64(-38)            // -ENOSYS

SYS_WRITE. Looks up `fd` in the process's POSIX fd table and
dispatches by kind. STDOUT/STDERR take the direct UART fast-path
via the arch-provided `shim_uart_putc`; TCP sockets chunk at
`INET_MAX_TCP_SEG=480` and loop until the full payload is
buffered. UDP sockets are rejected (use `sendto`). Any FREE slot
→ -EBADF. The user's buffer lives in the current ptbr which the
kernel inherits, so pointers dereference directly without
vm_copy_from.

    sys_write(fd: int, buf: i64, len: int) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_STDOUT || k == POSIX_FD_STDERR
            var p: *byte = *byte(buf)
            var i = 0
            while i < len
                shim_uart_putc(int(p[i]))
                i += 1
            return i64(len)
        if k == POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            val inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
            if inet_fd < 0
                return i64(-107)    // -ENOTCONN
            val port = inet_get_port()
            if port < 0
                return i64(-19)
            val ubuf: *byte = *byte(buf)
            var sent_total = 0
            while sent_total < len
                var chunk = len - sent_total
                if chunk > INET_MAX_TCP_SEG
                    chunk = INET_MAX_TCP_SEG
                var msg: [485]byte
                msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_SEND)
                shim_write_u16(msg, 1, inet_fd)
                shim_write_u16(msg, 3, chunk)
                var i = 0
                while i < chunk
                    msg[5 + i] = ubuf[sent_total + i]
                    i += 1
                var reply: [3]byte
                val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 5 + chunk, reply, 3)
                if r < 0
                    if sent_total > 0
                        return i64(sent_total)
                    return i64(-5)
                if reply[0] != 0
                    if sent_total > 0
                        return i64(sent_total)
                    return i64(-32)     // -EPIPE
                val n = shim_read_u16(reply, 1)
                if n <= 0
                    // Send buffer full. Non-blocking mode reports
                    // -EAGAIN if nothing has been queued yet; other-
                    // wise return the partial count so musl can
                    // decide whether to retry.
                    if sent_total == 0
                        if (posix_fd_flags(fd) & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK) != 0
                            return i64(-11)     // -EAGAIN
                    return i64(sent_total)
                sent_total += n
            return i64(sent_total)
        if k == POSIX_FD_FILE
            return shim_vfs_write(posix_fd_target(fd), buf, len)
        if k == POSIX_FD_EVENTFD
            // Linux requires len >= 8.
            if len < 8 || buf == i64(0)
                return i64(-22)             // -EINVAL
            val ubuf: *byte = *byte(buf)
            var add = i64(0)
            var bi = 0
            while bi < 8
                add = add | (i64(int(ubuf[bi]) & 0xff) << (bi * 8))
                bi += 1
            // Linux rejects the value u64::MAX (-1 as i64) — that
            // bit pattern is reserved as "invalid".
            if add == i64(-1)
                return i64(-22)
            val pid_w = posix_fd_current_pid()
            val widx = pid_w * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
            // Counter is unsigned u64-1 max; we treat i64 sign as
            // "high bit set" → near-overflow.  Accept the add if
            // current + add < 2^64 - 1.  Practically: detect
            // overflow on signed addition by checking whether the
            // sum wraps below either operand (works because we
            // never exceed i64::MAX before this point).
            val cur = posix_fd_table[widx].eventfd_count
            val sum = cur + add
            // Overflow detection — same operands both unsigned-wise.
            if u64(sum) < u64(cur)
                if (posix_fd_flags(fd) & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK) != 0
                    return i64(-11)         // -EAGAIN
                // Blocking mode: slix has no inter-thread notify
                // path that wakes a write blocked on counter
                // headroom yet, so return -EAGAIN even in blocking
                // mode (matches musl when no waiter exists). Real
                // behaviour for the threaded "writer waits for
                // reader" case lands with the inter-thread notify
                // chunk.
                return i64(-11)
            posix_fd_table[widx].eventfd_count = sum
            return i64(8)
        i64(-9)             // -EBADF

SYS_READ. STDIN returns 0 (EOF) — no kernel-side keyboard path
yet; TTY routing is on the bridge memory's todo list. TCP sockets
block in the inet server until data arrives or peer FINs. Write-
only STDIO kinds and UDP (which should use recvfrom) are rejected.
File-kind reads (`POSIX_FD_FILE`) forward to VFS, which dispatches
on its OFT type — files seek server-side, pipes block, TTY
handles read from the TTY driver.

    sys_read(fd: int, buf: i64, len: int) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_STDIN
            // Route through the TTY server. Currently every slix
            // process is bound to console 0 — multi-tty isn't a
            // thing yet. Returns 1 byte per call (TTY_CMD_READ
            // hands back one char); musl's read loops as needed.
            // The tty server blocks until a key arrives, so a
            // blocking read here just defers to that wake path.
            // Non-blocking stdin (O_NONBLOCK on fd 0) isn't yet
            // honoured — stdin is mostly used line-edit-style
            // anyway; epoll on stdin works through TTY_CMD_POLL.
            if len <= 0
                return i64(0)
            if buf == i64(0)
                return i64(-14)
            val port = tty_get_port()
            if port < 0
                return i64(-19)
            var msg: [2]byte
            msg[0] = byte(TTY_CMD_READ_C)
            msg[1] = byte(0)
            var reply: [2]byte
            val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 2, reply, 2)
            if r < 0
                return i64(-5)
            if reply[0] != 0
                return i64(-5)
            val ubuf: *byte = *byte(buf)
            ubuf[0] = reply[1]
            return i64(1)
        if k == POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            val inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
            if inet_fd < 0
                return i64(-107)
            if len <= 0
                return i64(0)
            val port = inet_get_port()
            if port < 0
                return i64(-19)
            var cap_len = len
            if cap_len > INET_MAX_TCP_SEG
                cap_len = INET_MAX_TCP_SEG
            val nb = posix_fd_flags(fd) & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
            var msg: [5]byte
            if nb != 0
                msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_RECV_NB)
            else
                msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_RECV)
            shim_write_u16(msg, 1, inet_fd)
            shim_write_u16(msg, 3, cap_len)
            var reply: [540]byte
            val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 5, reply, 540)
            if r < 0
                return i64(-5)
            if nb != 0
                val raw = int(reply[0]) & 0xff
                if raw != 0
                    if raw >= 128
                        return i64(raw - 256)
                    return i64(raw)
            if reply[0] != 0
                return i64(-5)
            val rlen = shim_read_u16(reply, 1)
            val ubuf: *byte = *byte(buf)
            var i = 0
            while i < rlen
                ubuf[i] = reply[3 + i]
                i += 1
            return i64(rlen)
        if k == POSIX_FD_FILE
            return shim_vfs_read(posix_fd_target(fd), buf, len)
        if k == POSIX_FD_TIMERFD
            if len < 8 || buf == i64(0)
                return i64(-22)
            val pid_t = posix_fd_current_pid()
            val tidx = pid_t * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
            timerfd_advance(tidx)
            val cnt = posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_expirations
            if cnt == i64(0)
                if (posix_fd_flags(fd) & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK) != 0
                    return i64(-11)
                // Blocking read on timerfd needs an in-shim sleep
                // loop until the next deadline. Simpler than the
                // socket case since we know exactly when to wake.
                // Loop: while expirations==0, sleep_or_notify until
                // next_tick. notify_wait() if disarmed (would block
                // forever).
                while posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_expirations == i64(0)
                    if posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_next_tick == 0
                        // Disarmed timer in blocking mode: Linux blocks
                        // forever waiting for someone else to settime.
                        // Slix has no concurrent settime path yet, so
                        // EAGAIN here matches the practical behaviour.
                        return i64(-11)
                    val now_b = int(uptime())
                    val wait_ticks = posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_next_tick - now_b
                    if wait_ticks > 0
                        sleep_or_notify(wait_ticks)
                    timerfd_advance(tidx)
            val deliver = posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_expirations
            posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_expirations = i64(0)
            val ubuf: *byte = *byte(buf)
            var bi = 0
            while bi < 8
                ubuf[bi] = byte(int((deliver >> i64(bi * 8))) & 0xff)
                bi += 1
            return i64(8)
        if k == POSIX_FD_EVENTFD
            if len < 8 || buf == i64(0)
                return i64(-22)
            val pid_r = posix_fd_current_pid()
            val ridx = pid_r * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
            val cur = posix_fd_table[ridx].eventfd_count
            if cur == i64(0)
                if (posix_fd_flags(fd) & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK) != 0
                    return i64(-11)         // -EAGAIN
                // Blocking semantics: see write-side note above —
                // slix doesn't yet have an inter-thread notify
                // for eventfd, so return -EAGAIN in blocking mode
                // too. Single-threaded user code that writes
                // before reading hits the cur != 0 path naturally.
                return i64(-11)
            // Semaphore mode hands back 1 per read; default mode
            // returns the full counter and resets to 0.
            var deliver = cur
            var remain = i64(0)
            if posix_fd_table[ridx].eventfd_semaphore != 0
                deliver = i64(1)
                remain = cur - i64(1)
            posix_fd_table[ridx].eventfd_count = remain
            val ubuf: *byte = *byte(buf)
            var bi = 0
            while bi < 8
                ubuf[bi] = byte(int((deliver >> i64(bi * 8))) & 0xff)
                bi += 1
            return i64(8)
        i64(-9)             // -EBADF

VFS read/write helpers. Both create a grant against the user's
buffer (granter_pid = current pid), send the appropriate VFS
command, and revoke the grant on the way out. The shim is in
kernel mode with the user's PT active, so `u64(buf)` is a valid
user vaddr that VFS can resolve via `svc_grant_map`. Length is
unbounded — VFS streams as much as it has buffered and reports
the actual count.

    shim_vfs_read(handle: int, buf: i64, len: int) -> i64
        if len <= 0
            return i64(0)
        if handle < 0
            return i64(-9)      // -EBADF
        val port = vfs_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-19)     // -ENODEV
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val gid = kernel_grant_create_inline(pid, u64(buf), len, SHIM_GRANT_WRITE)
        if gid < 0
            return i64(-12)     // -ENOMEM
        var msg: [13]byte
        msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_READ_C)
        shim_write_be_i32(msg, 1, handle)
        shim_write_be_i32(msg, 5, len)
        shim_write_be_i32(msg, 9, gid)
        var reply: [5]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 13, reply, 5)
        kernel_grant_revoke_inline(pid, gid)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-5)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-5)
        i64(shim_read_be_i32(reply, 1))

    shim_vfs_write(handle: int, buf: i64, len: int) -> i64
        if len <= 0
            return i64(0)
        if handle < 0
            return i64(-9)
        val port = vfs_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-19)
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val gid = kernel_grant_create_inline(pid, u64(buf), len, SHIM_GRANT_READ)
        if gid < 0
            return i64(-12)
        var msg: [13]byte
        msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_WRITE_C)
        shim_write_be_i32(msg, 1, handle)
        shim_write_be_i32(msg, 5, len)
        shim_write_be_i32(msg, 9, gid)
        var reply: [5]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 13, reply, 5)
        kernel_grant_revoke_inline(pid, gid)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-5)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-5)
        i64(shim_read_be_i32(reply, 1))

SYS_CLOSE. For socket kinds, tell the inet server to drop the
backend record; the fd slot is cleared either way so a subsequent
op returns -EBADF. Pre-connect / pre-listen TCP sockets (target
still -1) skip the inet call — no backend was ever allocated.

    sys_close(fd: int) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_FREE
            return i64(-9)  // -EBADF
        val target = posix_fd_target(fd)
        // If this fd was duped, another slot still owns the inet
        // record — clear our slot but leave the backend alone.
        val has_dup = posix_fd_other_dup_exists(fd, k, target)
        if has_dup == 0
            if k == POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
                val port = inet_get_port()
                if port >= 0
                    var msg: [2]byte
                    msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_CLOSE)
                    msg[1] = byte(target & 0xff)
                    var reply: [1]byte
                    ipc_send(port, msg, 2, reply, 1)
            if k == POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                if target >= 0
                    val port = inet_get_port()
                    if port >= 0
                        // SO_LINGER l_onoff=1 + l_linger=0: send RST
                        // instead of FIN (Linux's "abort close"). Any
                        // other linger configuration falls through to
                        // the standard FIN close — flush-or-timeout
                        // semantics aren't enforced yet.
                        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
                        val cidx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
                        val linger_on = posix_fd_table[cidx].linger_on
                        val linger_secs = posix_fd_table[cidx].linger_secs
                        var msg: [3]byte
                        if linger_on != 0 && linger_secs == 0
                            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_ABORT_CLOSE)
                        else
                            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_CLOSE)
                        shim_write_u16(msg, 1, target)
                        var reply: [1]byte
                        ipc_send(port, msg, 3, reply, 1)
            if k == POSIX_FD_FILE
                val port = vfs_get_port()
                if port >= 0
                    var msg: [5]byte
                    msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_CLOSE_C)
                    shim_write_be_i32(msg, 1, target)
                    var reply: [1]byte
                    ipc_send(port, msg, 5, reply, 1)
            if k == POSIX_FD_EPOLL
                epoll_inst_close_all(target)
                epoll_inst_release(target)
        posix_fd_clear(fd)
        i64(0)

SYS_SOCKET. Supports AF_INET + SOCK_DGRAM/SOCK_STREAM. Datagram
sockets eagerly ask inet for a UDP slot. Stream sockets return an
unconnected POSIX fd (target = -1); the inet-side socket is
allocated lazily by `sys_connect` or `sys_listen` so we don't have
to map a "pre-connect" state into the inet protocol.

    sys_socket(family: int, stype: int, proto: int) -> i64
        if family != POSIX_AF_INET
            return i64(-97)     // -EAFNOSUPPORT
        if stype == POSIX_SOCK_DGRAM
            val port = inet_get_port()
            if port < 0
                return i64(-19)     // -ENODEV
            var msg: [4]byte
            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_SOCKET)
            msg[1] = byte(family & 0xff)
            msg[2] = byte(stype & 0xff)
            msg[3] = byte(proto & 0xff)
            var reply: [2]byte
            val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 4, reply, 2)
            if r < 0
                return i64(-5)      // -EIO
            if reply[0] != 0
                return i64(-5)
            val inet_fd = int(reply[1]) & 0xff
            val posix_fd = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET, inet_fd)
            if posix_fd < 0
                var close_msg: [2]byte
                close_msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_CLOSE)
                close_msg[1] = byte(inet_fd & 0xff)
                var close_rep: [1]byte
                ipc_send(port, close_msg, 2, close_rep, 1)
                return i64(-24)     // -EMFILE
            return i64(posix_fd)
        if stype == POSIX_SOCK_STREAM
            val posix_fd = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET, -1)
            if posix_fd < 0
                return i64(-24)
            return i64(posix_fd)
        i64(-93)                    // -EPROTONOSUPPORT

SYS_BIND. For UDP sockets, forward to inet's BIND. For TCP
sockets, stash the bound port in `slot.pos` so a subsequent
`listen()` can use it — inet allocates the actual listen slot
lazily in `sys_listen`. The user's `sockaddr_in` is 16 bytes:
family(2), port(2, BE), addr(4), zero(8).

    sys_bind(fd: int, addr_ptr: i64, addrlen: int) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if addrlen < 16
            return i64(-22)
        val a: *byte = *byte(addr_ptr)
        val sin_port = shim_read_u16(a, 2)
        if k == POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
            posix_fd_table[pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd].pos = i64(sin_port)
            return i64(0)
        if k != POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            return i64(-9)
        val inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-19)
        var msg: [9]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_BIND)
        msg[1] = byte(inet_fd & 0xff)
        msg[2] = byte(POSIX_AF_INET)
        msg[3] = byte(sin_port >> 8)
        msg[4] = byte(sin_port & 0xff)
        msg[5] = a[4]
        msg[6] = a[5]
        msg[7] = a[6]
        msg[8] = a[7]
        var reply: [1]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 9, reply, 1)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-5)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-98)     // -EADDRINUSE
        i64(0)

SYS_SENDTO. Args: (fd, buf, len, flags, dst_addr, addrlen).
Copies the payload into an in-kernel staging buffer (avoids
re-reading user memory during ipc_deliver's cross-PT walk) and
forwards to the inet server. `flags` is ignored for v1.

    sys_sendto(fd: int, buf: i64, len: int, flags: int, addr_ptr: i64, addrlen: int) -> i64
        if posix_fd_kind(fd) != POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            return i64(-9)
        if addrlen < 16
            return i64(-22)
        if len < 0
            return i64(-22)
        if len > 512
            return i64(-90)     // -EMSGSIZE
        val inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-19)
        val a: *byte = *byte(addr_ptr)
        val dst_port = shim_read_u16(a, 2)
        val ubuf: *byte = *byte(buf)
        var msg: [523]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_SENDTO)
        msg[1] = byte(inet_fd & 0xff)
        msg[2] = byte(POSIX_AF_INET)
        msg[3] = byte(dst_port >> 8)
        msg[4] = byte(dst_port & 0xff)
        msg[5] = a[4]
        msg[6] = a[5]
        msg[7] = a[6]
        msg[8] = a[7]
        msg[9] = byte((len >> 8) & 0xff)
        msg[10] = byte(len & 0xff)
        var i = 0
        while i < len
            msg[11 + i] = ubuf[i]
            i += 1
        var reply: [3]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 11 + len, reply, 3)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-5)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-101)    // -ENETUNREACH
        i64(shim_read_u16(reply, 1))

SYS_RECVFROM. Args: (fd, buf, len, flags, src_addr, addrlen_ptr).
Blocks until a datagram is available. Writes the source's family
+ port + addr into `src_addr` (16-byte sockaddr_in) if non-null,
updates `*addrlen_ptr` to 16, and copies the payload into `buf`.

    sys_recvfrom(fd: int, buf: i64, len: int, flags: int, addr_ptr: i64, addrlen_ptr: i64) -> i64
        if posix_fd_kind(fd) != POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            return i64(-9)
        if len < 0
            return i64(-22)
        val inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-19)
        var cap_len = len
        if cap_len > 512
            cap_len = 512
        val nonblock = posix_fd_flags(fd) & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
        var reply: [540]byte
        if nonblock != 0
            // Non-blocking: reuse RECVFROM_TIMEOUT with a zero
            // timeout — the inet handler will reply with status=1
            // (no data) immediately if the queue is empty.
            var nmsg: [8]byte
            nmsg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_RECVFROM_TIMEOUT)
            nmsg[1] = byte(inet_fd & 0xff)
            nmsg[2] = byte((cap_len >> 8) & 0xff)
            nmsg[3] = byte(cap_len & 0xff)
            nmsg[4] = byte(0)
            nmsg[5] = byte(0)
            nmsg[6] = byte(0)
            nmsg[7] = byte(0)
            val nr = ipc_send(port, nmsg, 8, reply, 540)
            if nr < 0
                return i64(-5)
            if reply[0] == byte(1)
                return i64(-11)         // -EAGAIN
            if reply[0] == byte(2)
                return i64(-111)        // -ECONNREFUSED (ICMP error)
            if reply[0] != 0
                return i64(-5)
        else
            var msg: [4]byte
            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_RECVFROM)
            msg[1] = byte(inet_fd & 0xff)
            msg[2] = byte((cap_len >> 8) & 0xff)
            msg[3] = byte(cap_len & 0xff)
            val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 4, reply, 540)
            if r < 0
                return i64(-5)
            if reply[0] == byte(2)
                return i64(-111)        // -ECONNREFUSED (ICMP error)
            if reply[0] != 0
                return i64(-5)
        val src_port = shim_read_u16(reply, 2)
        val payload_len = shim_read_u16(reply, 8)
        if addr_ptr != i64(0)
            val a: *byte = *byte(addr_ptr)
            a[0] = byte(POSIX_AF_INET & 0xff)
            a[1] = byte((POSIX_AF_INET >> 8) & 0xff)
            a[2] = byte(src_port >> 8)
            a[3] = byte(src_port & 0xff)
            a[4] = reply[4]
            a[5] = reply[5]
            a[6] = reply[6]
            a[7] = reply[7]
            var z = 8
            while z < 16
                a[z] = byte(0)
                z += 1
        if addrlen_ptr != i64(0)
            val alp: *i32 = *i32(addrlen_ptr)
            *alp = i32(16)
        val ubuf: *byte = *byte(buf)
        var copy_len = payload_len
        if copy_len > len
            copy_len = len
        var i = 0
        while i < copy_len
            ubuf[i] = reply[10 + i]
            i += 1
        i64(copy_len)

SYS_SENDMSG / SYS_RECVMSG. MVP that handles `msg_iov` gather/
scatter and `msg_name` for UDP datagrams. Ancillary data
(`msg_control`) is ignored — programs that pass control buffers
won't observe them coming back. `msg_flags` is currently a
no-op; O_NONBLOCK still comes from the fd's flags via fcntl.

LP64 little-endian struct layout (matches musl's `<sys/socket.h>`
on both aarch64 and x86_64):

```
struct msghdr {
    void         *msg_name;        // off  0, 8 bytes
    socklen_t     msg_namelen;     // off  8, 4 bytes (+ 4 pad)
    struct iovec *msg_iov;         // off 16, 8 bytes
    int           msg_iovlen;      // off 24, 4 bytes (+ 4 __pad1)
    void         *msg_control;     // off 32, 8 bytes
    socklen_t     msg_controllen;  // off 40, 4 bytes (+ 4 pad)
    int           msg_flags;       // off 48, 4 bytes
};
struct iovec { void *iov_base; size_t iov_len; }; // 16 bytes
```

To keep the gather/scatter buffer bounded, we cap `iovlen` at
`SHIM_IOV_MAX = 8` and total bytes at `SHIM_MSG_MAX = 512` —
matches the inet UDP datagram cap and is plenty for the TCP
chunk-at-a-time path.

    val SHIM_IOV_MAX        = 8
    val SHIM_MSG_MAX        = 512

    shim_read_ptr(p: *byte, off: int) -> i64
        var v: i64 = 0
        var i = 0
        while i < 8
            v = v | (i64(int(p[off + i]) & 0xff) << (i * 8))
            i += 1
        v

    shim_read_i32(p: *byte, off: int) -> int
        (int(p[off]) & 0xff) | ((int(p[off + 1]) & 0xff) << 8) | ((int(p[off + 2]) & 0xff) << 16) | ((int(p[off + 3]) & 0xff) << 24)

    shim_write_i32(p: *byte, off: int, v: int)
        p[off]     = byte(v & 0xff)
        p[off + 1] = byte((v >> 8) & 0xff)
        p[off + 2] = byte((v >> 16) & 0xff)
        p[off + 3] = byte((v >> 24) & 0xff)

    sys_sendmsg(fd: int, msghdr_ptr: i64, flags: int) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k != POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                return i64(-9)
        if msghdr_ptr == i64(0)
            return i64(-14)
        val mh: *byte = *byte(msghdr_ptr)
        val name_ptr  = shim_read_ptr(mh, 0)
        val name_len  = shim_read_i32(mh, 8)
        val iov_ptr   = shim_read_ptr(mh, 16)
        val iov_len   = shim_read_i32(mh, 24)
        if iov_len < 0
            return i64(-22)
        if iov_len > SHIM_IOV_MAX
            return i64(-22)
        if iov_len > 0
            if iov_ptr == i64(0)
                return i64(-14)
        var gather: [512]byte
        var total = 0
        val iov_base: *byte = *byte(iov_ptr)
        var ii = 0
        while ii < iov_len
            val ent_off = ii * 16
            val base    = shim_read_ptr(iov_base, ent_off)
            val seg_len = int(shim_read_ptr(iov_base, ent_off + 8))
            if seg_len > 0
                if base == i64(0)
                    return i64(-14)
                val src: *byte = *byte(base)
                var jj = 0
                while jj < seg_len
                    if total < SHIM_MSG_MAX
                        gather[total] = src[jj]
                        total += 1
                    jj += 1
            ii += 1
        if k == POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            if name_ptr == i64(0)
                return i64(-22)
            if name_len < 16
                return i64(-22)
            return sys_sendto(fd, i64(&gather[0]), total, flags, name_ptr, 16)
        sys_write(fd, i64(&gather[0]), total)

    sys_recvmsg(fd: int, msghdr_ptr: i64, flags: int) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k != POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                return i64(-9)
        if msghdr_ptr == i64(0)
            return i64(-14)
        val mh: *byte = *byte(msghdr_ptr)
        val name_ptr  = shim_read_ptr(mh, 0)
        val iov_ptr   = shim_read_ptr(mh, 16)
        val iov_len   = shim_read_i32(mh, 24)
        if iov_len < 0
            return i64(-22)
        if iov_len > SHIM_IOV_MAX
            return i64(-22)
        if iov_len > 0
            if iov_ptr == i64(0)
                return i64(-14)
        // Compute total scatter capacity across iovs.
        var cap = 0
        val iov_base: *byte = *byte(iov_ptr)
        var ii = 0
        while ii < iov_len
            val ent_off = ii * 16
            val seg_len = int(shim_read_ptr(iov_base, ent_off + 8))
            if seg_len > 0
                cap += seg_len
            ii += 1
        if cap > SHIM_MSG_MAX
            cap = SHIM_MSG_MAX
        var scratch: [512]byte
        // Stage the receive into the scratch buffer; sys_recvfrom
        // / sys_read writes there, then we scatter into the iovs.
        var got: i64 = i64(0)
        var src_addr: [16]byte
        var src_len: i32 = i32(16)
        if k == POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            // For UDP, write src into msg_name if the user gave one.
            if name_ptr != i64(0)
                got = sys_recvfrom(fd, i64(&scratch[0]), cap, flags,
                    name_ptr, msghdr_ptr + i64(8))
            else
                got = sys_recvfrom(fd, i64(&scratch[0]), cap, flags,
                    i64(&src_addr[0]), i64(&src_len))
        else
            got = sys_read(fd, i64(&scratch[0]), cap)
        if got < i64(0)
            return got
        // Scatter into iovs.
        val nbytes = int(got)
        var written = 0
        ii = 0
        while ii < iov_len
            val ent_off = ii * 16
            val base    = shim_read_ptr(iov_base, ent_off)
            val seg_len = int(shim_read_ptr(iov_base, ent_off + 8))
            if seg_len > 0
                if base != i64(0)
                    val dst: *byte = *byte(base)
                    var take = nbytes - written
                    if take > seg_len
                        take = seg_len
                    var jj = 0
                    while jj < take
                        dst[jj] = scratch[written + jj]
                        jj += 1
                    written += take
            ii += 1
        // Clear msg_controllen and msg_flags in the user's struct
        // (signal "no ancillary, no flags" cleanly).
        shim_write_i32(mh, 40, 0)
        shim_write_i32(mh, 48, 0)
        i64(nbytes)

SYS_CONNECT. TCP active open. Issues `INET_CMD_TCP_CONNECT` which
parks the caller until ESTABLISHED; on success the inet server
replies with its socket id. We stash that id as the slot's backend
target so subsequent read/write/close route to the right TCP
record. The user's sockaddr_in gives dst_ip (4 bytes) and
dst_port (BE u16) at the usual offsets.

    sys_connect(fd: int, addr_ptr: i64, addrlen: int) -> i64
        if posix_fd_kind(fd) != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            return i64(-9)
        if addrlen < 16
            return i64(-22)
        if posix_fd_target(fd) >= 0
            return i64(-56)             // -EISCONN
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-19)
        val a: *byte = *byte(addr_ptr)
        val nb = posix_fd_flags(fd) & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val idx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
        val pending_snd = posix_fd_table[idx].pending_sndbuf
        val pending_rcv = posix_fd_table[idx].pending_rcvbuf
        var msg: [11]byte
        if nb != 0
            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_CONNECT_NB)
        else
            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_CONNECT)
        msg[1] = a[4]
        msg[2] = a[5]
        msg[3] = a[6]
        msg[4] = a[7]
        msg[5] = a[2]                   // sin_port high byte (already BE)
        msg[6] = a[3]                   // sin_port low byte
        // SO_SNDBUF / SO_RCVBUF the caller stashed pre-connect via
        // setsockopt. 0 means "use the inet-side default".
        var snd_clamped = pending_snd
        if snd_clamped > 0xffff
            snd_clamped = 0xffff
        var rcv_clamped = pending_rcv
        if rcv_clamped > 0xffff
            rcv_clamped = 0xffff
        shim_write_u16(msg, 7, snd_clamped)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 9, rcv_clamped)
        var reply: [2]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 11, reply, 2)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-5)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-111)            // -ECONNREFUSED
        val inet_fd = int(reply[1]) & 0xff
        posix_fd_table[idx].target = inet_fd
        // Apply pending SO_KEEPALIVE now that the slot exists.
        // Bufsize already shipped through the extended CONNECT
        // wire; keepalive uses a follow-up IPC since it's a
        // post-handshake property (only fires in ESTABLISHED).
        if posix_fd_table[idx].flag_keepalive != 0
            shim_inet_set_keepalive(inet_fd, 1)
        // Apply persisted TCP_KEEPIDLE / TCP_KEEPINTVL / TCP_KEEPCNT.
        // Same shape as keepalive: pre-slot setsockopt landed only
        // on the shim, so the slot starts with defaults until we
        // replay the per-option IPC here.
        if posix_fd_table[idx].keep_idle_secs > 0
            shim_inet_set_keepopt(inet_fd, 0,
                posix_fd_table[idx].keep_idle_secs * 100)
        if posix_fd_table[idx].keep_intvl_secs > 0
            shim_inet_set_keepopt(inet_fd, 1,
                posix_fd_table[idx].keep_intvl_secs * 100)
        if posix_fd_table[idx].keep_cnt > 0
            shim_inet_set_keepopt(inet_fd, 2,
                posix_fd_table[idx].keep_cnt)
        // Non-blocking connect: surface -EINPROGRESS so the user
        // can poll EPOLLOUT for completion. The slot is already
        // SYN_SENT; once it reaches ESTABLISHED the existing
        // inet_epoll_fire path delivers the readiness wake.
        if nb != 0
            return i64(-115)            // -EINPROGRESS
        i64(0)

SYS_LISTEN. Requires a prior bind() to have set the local port in
`slot.pos`. Forwards to `INET_CMD_TCP_LISTEN` and stashes the
returned listen fd as the backend target. `backlog` is advisory —
inet uses a fixed `TCP_LISTEN_BACKLOG` internally.

    sys_listen(fd: int, backlog: int) -> i64
        if posix_fd_kind(fd) != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            return i64(-9)
        if posix_fd_target(fd) >= 0
            return i64(-22)             // already listening or connected
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val idx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
        val local_port = int(posix_fd_table[idx].pos)
        if local_port <= 0
            return i64(-22)             // -EINVAL — must bind first
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-19)
        var msg: [9]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_LISTEN)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 1, local_port)
        // Pass user's `backlog` through; inet caps at TCP_LISTEN_BACKLOG
        // and normalises 0 to 1. Negative values are clamped to 1 too.
        var bl = backlog
        if bl < 0
            bl = 0
        if bl > 0xffff
            bl = 0xffff
        shim_write_u16(msg, 3, bl)
        // Pre-listen SO_SNDBUF / SO_RCVBUF — accepted children inherit
        // the listening socket's sizes (Linux semantics).
        var snd_clamped = posix_fd_table[idx].pending_sndbuf
        if snd_clamped > 0xffff
            snd_clamped = 0xffff
        var rcv_clamped = posix_fd_table[idx].pending_rcvbuf
        if rcv_clamped > 0xffff
            rcv_clamped = 0xffff
        shim_write_u16(msg, 5, snd_clamped)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 7, rcv_clamped)
        var reply: [2]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 9, reply, 2)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-5)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-98)             // -EADDRINUSE
        val listen_fd = int(reply[1]) & 0xff
        posix_fd_table[idx].target = listen_fd
        // Apply pending SO_KEEPALIVE — accepted children inherit
        // the listener's flag via inet's handle_passive_syn.
        if posix_fd_table[idx].flag_keepalive != 0
            shim_inet_set_keepalive(listen_fd, 1)
        // Apply pending TCP_KEEPIDLE / TCP_KEEPINTVL / TCP_KEEPCNT —
        // accepted children also inherit these via
        // inet_tcp_handle_passive_syn.
        if posix_fd_table[idx].keep_idle_secs > 0
            shim_inet_set_keepopt(listen_fd, 0,
                posix_fd_table[idx].keep_idle_secs * 100)
        if posix_fd_table[idx].keep_intvl_secs > 0
            shim_inet_set_keepopt(listen_fd, 1,
                posix_fd_table[idx].keep_intvl_secs * 100)
        if posix_fd_table[idx].keep_cnt > 0
            shim_inet_set_keepopt(listen_fd, 2,
                posix_fd_table[idx].keep_cnt)
        i64(0)

SYS_ACCEPT. Blocks until a child connection arrives unless the
listen fd is `O_NONBLOCK`, in which case the inet NB variant
returns -EAGAIN immediately when the queue is empty. inet replies
with the child's socket id + peer's ip/port; we allocate a fresh
POSIX slot for the child, populate `*addr` with the peer's
sockaddr_in (if non-null), and return the new POSIX fd. Inet may
also send data + FIN before accept returns, so the child can be in
CLOSE_WAIT on arrival — read/close paths handle that already.

    sys_accept(fd: int, addr_ptr: i64, addrlen_ptr: i64) -> i64
        if posix_fd_kind(fd) != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            return i64(-9)
        val listen_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
        if listen_fd < 0
            return i64(-22)
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-19)
        val nb = posix_fd_flags(fd) & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
        var msg: [3]byte
        if nb != 0
            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_ACCEPT_NB)
        else
            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_ACCEPT)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 1, listen_fd)
        var reply: [8]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 3, reply, 8)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-5)
        if reply[0] == byte(-11)
            // NB variant returns -EAGAIN when queue is empty.
            return i64(-11)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-5)
        val child_inet_fd = int(reply[1]) & 0xff
        val peer_port = shim_read_u16(reply, 6)
        if addr_ptr != i64(0)
            val a: *byte = *byte(addr_ptr)
            a[0] = byte(POSIX_AF_INET & 0xff)
            a[1] = byte(0)
            a[2] = byte(peer_port >> 8)
            a[3] = byte(peer_port & 0xff)
            a[4] = reply[2]
            a[5] = reply[3]
            a[6] = reply[4]
            a[7] = reply[5]
            var z = 8
            while z < 16
                a[z] = byte(0)
                z += 1
        if addrlen_ptr != i64(0)
            val alp: *i32 = *i32(addrlen_ptr)
            *alp = i32(16)
        val new_fd = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET, child_inet_fd)
        if new_fd < 0
            var close_msg: [3]byte
            close_msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_CLOSE)
            shim_write_u16(close_msg, 1, child_inet_fd)
            var close_rep: [1]byte
            ipc_send(port, close_msg, 3, close_rep, 1)
            return i64(-24)             // -EMFILE
        i64(new_fd)

SYS_ACCEPT4.  Linux's accept(2) variant that takes a `flags`
argument so the accepted child fd can be born non-blocking
(`SOCK_NONBLOCK = 0x800` = same value as `O_NONBLOCK`) and/or
close-on-exec (`SOCK_CLOEXEC = 0x80000`) atomically — without
the racy `accept` + `fcntl(F_SETFL, O_NONBLOCK)` dance servers
otherwise have to do.  The shim is single-threaded so the race
isn't observable here either way, but matching the contract
keeps libuv / Node.js / glibc happy.

`SOCK_NONBLOCK` is honoured: the child PosixFd carries
`POSIX_O_NONBLOCK` from birth, so its first read/write/etc.
already routes through the non-blocking inet path.
`SOCK_CLOEXEC` is accept-and-ignore for now (no fork/exec
story yet — see `feedback_no_workarounds_build_it.md` for the
follow-up arc).  Unknown flag bits return -EINVAL.

    val POSIX_SOCK_NONBLOCK = 2048
    val POSIX_SOCK_CLOEXEC  = 524288

    sys_accept4(fd: int, addr_ptr: i64, addrlen_ptr: i64, flags: int) -> i64
        val known = POSIX_SOCK_NONBLOCK | POSIX_SOCK_CLOEXEC
        if (flags & ~known) != 0
            return i64(-22)             // -EINVAL
        val r = sys_accept(fd, addr_ptr, addrlen_ptr)
        if r < i64(0)
            return r
        val new_fd = int(r)
        if (flags & POSIX_SOCK_NONBLOCK) != 0
            val cur = posix_fd_flags(new_fd)
            posix_fd_set_flags(new_fd, cur | POSIX_O_NONBLOCK)
        i64(new_fd)


SYS_SHUTDOWN. Real half-close via `INET_CMD_TCP_SHUTDOWN`. `how`
values (Linux): 0 = SHUT_RD (no-op — we don't track a read-shut
state, reads continue), 1 = SHUT_WR (inet emits FIN via
`inet_tcp_do_shutdown_wr`, leaves socket readable until peer
FINs, does NOT park the caller), 2 = SHUT_RDWR (treated as
SHUT_WR for now — a proper SHUT_RDWR would also drop buffered
recv data). Not valid on non-TCP fds.

    sys_shutdown(fd: int, how: int) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            return i64(-9)
        val inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
        if inet_fd < 0
            return i64(-107)            // -ENOTCONN
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-19)
        var msg: [4]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_SHUTDOWN)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 1, inet_fd)
        msg[3] = byte(how & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 4, reply, 1)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-5)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-5)
        i64(0)

Send INET_CMD_TCP_SET_KEEPALIVE.  Returns 0 / negative errno.
Used at setsockopt(SO_KEEPALIVE) time on a TCP fd that already
has an inet slot, and at connect/listen time to apply a flag the
caller stashed pre-slot.

    shim_inet_set_keepalive(inet_fd: int, on: int) -> int
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return -5
        var msg: [4]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_SET_KEEPALIVE)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 1, inet_fd)
        msg[3] = byte(on & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 4, reply, 1)
        if r < 0
            return -5
        if reply[0] != 0
            return -22
        0


Send INET_CMD_TCP_SETBUF for one buffer.  `is_send` selects which.
Returns 0 on success, negative errno on failure.

    shim_inet_setbuf(inet_fd: int, is_send: bool, size: int) -> int
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return -5
        var msg: [6]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_SETBUF)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 1, inet_fd)
        if is_send
            msg[3] = byte(0)
        else
            msg[3] = byte(1)
        var sz = size
        if sz > 0xffff
            sz = 0xffff
        shim_write_u16(msg, 4, sz)
        var reply: [1]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 6, reply, 1)
        if r < 0
            return -5
        if reply[0] != 0
            return -22
        0

Send INET_CMD_TCP_SET_KEEPOPT.  `kind` selects IDLE (0), INTVL
(1), or CNT (2).  `value` is in *ticks* for IDLE/INTVL — this
helper does NOT do unit conversion; the caller has already
converted seconds→ticks. Returns 0 on success, negative errno on
failure.

    shim_inet_set_keepopt(inet_fd: int, kind: int, value: int) -> int
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return -5
        var msg: [8]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_SET_KEEPOPT)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 1, inet_fd)
        msg[3] = byte(kind & 0xff)
        msg[4] = byte((value >> 24) & 0xff)
        msg[5] = byte((value >> 16) & 0xff)
        msg[6] = byte((value >>  8) & 0xff)
        msg[7] = byte( value        & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 8, reply, 1)
        if r < 0
            return -5
        if reply[0] != 0
            return -22
        0


Send INET_CMD_TCP_GET_KEEPOPT.  Returns the per-socket value in
*ticks* (or count for CNT) on success via the `out v` parameter,
or a negative errno via the function return.  Caller-side does
the ticks→seconds conversion before handing back to userspace.

    shim_inet_get_keepopt(inet_fd: int, kind: int, out v: int) -> int
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return -5
        var msg: [4]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_GET_KEEPOPT)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 1, inet_fd)
        msg[3] = byte(kind & 0xff)
        var reply: [5]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 4, reply, 5)
        if r < 0
            return -5
        if reply[0] != 0
            return -22
        val b1 = (int(reply[1]) & 0xff) << 24
        val b2 = (int(reply[2]) & 0xff) << 16
        val b3 = (int(reply[3]) & 0xff) <<  8
        val b4 =  int(reply[4]) & 0xff
        v = b1 | b2 | b3 | b4
        0


SYS_SETSOCKOPT / SYS_GETSOCKOPT. The inet server tracks
`SO_SNDBUF` / `SO_RCVBUF` (since Phase E bufsize chunk).  The
remaining accept-list options (SO_REUSEADDR, SO_BROADCAST,
SO_KEEPALIVE, SO_REUSEPORT, TCP_NODELAY) are persisted on the
shim fd so getsockopt echoes the actually-set value (Linux-compat
introspection) but their behavioural enforcement is a follow-up:

- SO_REUSEADDR — slix is already permissive (no strict TIME_WAIT
  bind-block today), so the flag is informational.
- SO_BROADCAST — UDP send always allows broadcast destinations.
- SO_KEEPALIVE — no idle timer wired in inet yet.
- SO_REUSEPORT — same as REUSEADDR for the no-strict-bind case.
- TCP_NODELAY — slix doesn't implement Nagle, so segments already
  go out immediately; the flag is effectively pinned to "on".

Anything outside the accept-list returns `-ENOPROTOOPT`.

Linux `SOL_SOCKET = 1`; `SO_REUSEADDR = 2`, `SO_ERROR = 4`,
`SO_BROADCAST = 6`, `SO_SNDBUF = 7`, `SO_RCVBUF = 8`,
`SO_KEEPALIVE = 9`, `SO_REUSEPORT = 15`. `IPPROTO_TCP = 6`;
`TCP_NODELAY = 1`.

Read a 4-byte i32 out of the user's optval buffer.  Bounds + null-
pointer checks return non-zero on failure (caller should propagate
as -EINVAL).

    shim_sockopt_read_int(optval: i64, optlen: int, out v: int) -> int
        if optlen < 4 || optval == i64(0)
            return -1
        val vp: *i32 = *i32(optval)
        v = int(*vp)
        0

    sys_setsockopt(fd: int, level: int, optname: int, optval: i64, optlen: int) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k != POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                return i64(-9)          // -EBADF
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val tidx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
        if level == 1
            if optname == 2
                var v = 0
                if shim_sockopt_read_int(optval, optlen, v) != 0
                    return i64(-22)
                posix_fd_table[tidx].flag_reuseaddr = v
                return i64(0)
            if optname == 6
                var v = 0
                if shim_sockopt_read_int(optval, optlen, v) != 0
                    return i64(-22)
                posix_fd_table[tidx].flag_broadcast = v
                return i64(0)
            if optname == 9
                var v = 0
                if shim_sockopt_read_int(optval, optlen, v) != 0
                    return i64(-22)
                posix_fd_table[tidx].flag_keepalive = v
                // Push to inet immediately when a slot exists so
                // the probe/giveup cycle either starts (v != 0) or
                // stops (v == 0) right now. Pre-slot calls just
                // persist; sys_connect / sys_listen apply later.
                if k == POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                    val ka_inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
                    if ka_inet_fd >= 0
                        shim_inet_set_keepalive(ka_inet_fd, v)
                return i64(0)
            if optname == 15
                var v = 0
                if shim_sockopt_read_int(optval, optlen, v) != 0
                    return i64(-22)
                posix_fd_table[tidx].flag_reuseport = v
                return i64(0)
            // SO_LINGER. Wire is `struct linger { i32 l_onoff;
            // i32 l_linger; }` — 8 bytes.  Persist both fields;
            // sys_close consults them at close time to pick
            // FIN-vs-RST.  Negative l_linger is clamped to 0
            // (matches Linux glibc behaviour).
            if optname == 13
                if optlen < 8 || optval == i64(0)
                    return i64(-22)
                val lp_on: *i32 = *i32(optval)
                val lp_secs: *i32 = *i32(optval + i64(4))
                var l_on = int(*lp_on)
                var l_secs = int(*lp_secs)
                if l_secs < 0
                    l_secs = 0
                posix_fd_table[tidx].linger_on = l_on
                posix_fd_table[tidx].linger_secs = l_secs
                return i64(0)
            // SO_SNDBUF / SO_RCVBUF.  Persist on the shim fd so the
            // value survives until connect/listen creates an inet
            // slot, then apply via INET_CMD_TCP_SETBUF if a slot
            // already exists. UDP buffers aren't sized per-socket
            // today, so silently accept and ignore for UDP (matches
            // Linux's tolerance).
            if optname == 7 || optname == 8
                if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                    return i64(0)
                var requested = 0
                if shim_sockopt_read_int(optval, optlen, requested) != 0
                    return i64(-22)
                if requested < 0
                    requested = 0
                if optname == 7
                    posix_fd_table[tidx].pending_sndbuf = requested
                else
                    posix_fd_table[tidx].pending_rcvbuf = requested
                val inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
                if inet_fd < 0
                    return i64(0)
                val rc = shim_inet_setbuf(inet_fd, optname == 7, requested)
                if rc != 0
                    return i64(rc)
                return i64(0)
        if level == 6
            if optname == 1
                var v = 0
                if shim_sockopt_read_int(optval, optlen, v) != 0
                    return i64(-22)
                posix_fd_table[tidx].flag_nodelay = v
                return i64(0)
            // TCP_KEEPIDLE (4) / TCP_KEEPINTVL (5) — seconds.
            // TCP_KEEPCNT (6) — count.
            if optname == 4 || optname == 5 || optname == 6
                if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                    return i64(-92)
                var v = 0
                if shim_sockopt_read_int(optval, optlen, v) != 0
                    return i64(-22)
                if v < 0
                    v = 0
                if optname == 4
                    posix_fd_table[tidx].keep_idle_secs = v
                else if optname == 5
                    posix_fd_table[tidx].keep_intvl_secs = v
                else
                    posix_fd_table[tidx].keep_cnt = v
                // Push to inet immediately if a slot exists; pre-slot
                // calls just persist on the shim fd. Slot creation
                // (sys_connect / sys_listen) replays them via the
                // same path.
                val ko_inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
                if ko_inet_fd >= 0
                    var kind = 0
                    if optname == 5
                        kind = 1
                    else if optname == 6
                        kind = 2
                    var ticks = v
                    if optname != 6
                        ticks = v * 100        // sec → tick @ 100 Hz
                    val rc = shim_inet_set_keepopt(ko_inet_fd, kind, ticks)
                    if rc != 0
                        return i64(rc)
                return i64(0)
        i64(-92)                        // -ENOPROTOOPT

`val` is a pointer to the option buffer; `len_ptr` points at an
i32 that the caller seeded with the buffer capacity. We write 0
For SO_SNDBUF/SO_RCVBUF the value comes from inet (or the persisted
pending size when there's no slot yet).  For the other accept-list
flags (SO_REUSEADDR / SO_BROADCAST / SO_KEEPALIVE / SO_REUSEPORT /
TCP_NODELAY) we echo the actually-set value out of the shim fd
table.  SO_ERROR — which slix doesn't track per-socket — still
returns 0 (clean state).  `*len_ptr = 4` in every accepted case.
`-ENOPROTOOPT` is returned for anything outside the accept-list;
the option buffer is left untouched in that case.

    sys_getsockopt(fd: int, level: int, optname: int, optval: i64, len_ptr: i64) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k != POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                return i64(-9)          // -EBADF
        var accept = 0
        if level == 1
            if optname == 2
                accept = 1
            if optname == 4
                accept = 1
            if optname == 6
                accept = 1
            if optname == 7
                accept = 1
            if optname == 8
                accept = 1
            if optname == 9
                accept = 1
            if optname == 13
                accept = 1
            if optname == 15
                accept = 1
        if level == 6
            if optname == 1
                accept = 1
            if optname == 4 || optname == 5 || optname == 6
                accept = 1
        if accept == 0
            return i64(-92)             // -ENOPROTOOPT
        if len_ptr == i64(0)
            return i64(-14)             // -EFAULT
        val lp: *i32 = *i32(len_ptr)
        val cap = int(*lp)
        // SO_LINGER returns a struct linger (8 bytes); everything
        // else is an int (4 bytes).
        val need = if optname == 13 then 8 else 4
        if cap < need
            return i64(-22)             // -EINVAL
        if optval == i64(0)
            return i64(-14)
        // SO_LINGER: write back the persisted struct.  Pre-connect
        // / post-close fds carry the user's most recent set call
        // (or zero-init).  No inet round-trip needed since the
        // shim is the source of truth for linger state.
        if level == 1
            if optname == 13
                val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
                val lidx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
                val on_ptr: *i32 = *i32(optval)
                val secs_ptr: *i32 = *i32(optval + i64(4))
                *on_ptr = i32(posix_fd_table[lidx].linger_on)
                *secs_ptr = i32(posix_fd_table[lidx].linger_secs)
                *lp = i32(8)
                return i64(0)
        val vp: *i32 = *i32(optval)
        // SO_SNDBUF / SO_RCVBUF: ask the inet server for the actual
        // per-socket size.  Pre-connect TCP fds (no inet slot yet)
        // report the pending value the caller stashed via setsockopt
        // (or the inet-side default) so a setsockopt + getsockopt
        // round-trip on a fresh fd echoes the requested size — code
        // that introspects its own option ahead of bind/connect
        // wants a sane number.
        if level == 1
            if optname == 7 || optname == 8
                if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                    *vp = i32(0)
                    *lp = i32(4)
                    return i64(0)
                val inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
                if inet_fd < 0
                    val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
                    val idx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
                    var pending = 0
                    if optname == 7
                        pending = posix_fd_table[idx].pending_sndbuf
                        if pending == 0
                            pending = 4096
                    else
                        pending = posix_fd_table[idx].pending_rcvbuf
                        if pending == 0
                            pending = 1024
                    *vp = i32(pending)
                    *lp = i32(4)
                    return i64(0)
                val port = inet_get_port()
                if port < 0
                    return i64(-5)
                var msg: [4]byte
                msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_GETBUF)
                shim_write_u16(msg, 1, inet_fd)
                msg[3] = byte(0)
                if optname == 8
                    msg[3] = byte(1)
                var reply: [3]byte
                val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 4, reply, 3)
                if r < 0
                    return i64(-5)
                if reply[0] != 0
                    return i64(-22)
                *vp = i32(shim_read_u16(reply, 1))
                *lp = i32(4)
                return i64(0)
        // TCP_KEEPIDLE / TCP_KEEPINTVL / TCP_KEEPCNT.  Always answer
        // from the shim's persisted value — that's the source of
        // truth for what the user set, and round-tripping through
        // the inet ticks representation would lose the original
        // seconds (60 sec → 6000 ticks → 60 sec is fine, but small
        // values can drift if the user passes 0 / 1 in seconds and
        // we'd return the default rather than the persisted 0).
        if level == 6
            if optname == 4 || optname == 5 || optname == 6
                if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
                    *vp = i32(0)
                    *lp = i32(4)
                    return i64(0)
                val pid_k = posix_fd_current_pid()
                val kidx = pid_k * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
                var kv = 0
                if optname == 4
                    kv = posix_fd_table[kidx].keep_idle_secs
                else if optname == 5
                    kv = posix_fd_table[kidx].keep_intvl_secs
                else
                    kv = posix_fd_table[kidx].keep_cnt
                *vp = i32(kv)
                *lp = i32(4)
                return i64(0)
        // Echo the persisted flag value for the accept-list options
        // (SO_REUSEADDR / SO_BROADCAST / SO_KEEPALIVE / SO_REUSEPORT
        // / TCP_NODELAY).  SO_ERROR (4) has no per-socket state in
        // slix; it falls through with value 0 (clean state).
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val gidx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
        var echoed = 0
        if level == 1
            if optname == 2
                echoed = posix_fd_table[gidx].flag_reuseaddr
            if optname == 6
                echoed = posix_fd_table[gidx].flag_broadcast
            if optname == 9
                echoed = posix_fd_table[gidx].flag_keepalive
            if optname == 15
                echoed = posix_fd_table[gidx].flag_reuseport
        if level == 6
            if optname == 1
                echoed = posix_fd_table[gidx].flag_nodelay
        *vp = i32(echoed)
        *lp = i32(4)
        i64(0)

SYS_GETSOCKNAME / SYS_GETPEERNAME. Both return a 16-byte
`sockaddr_in` at `addr_ptr` and write the length (always 16)
into `*addrlen_ptr`. For UDP: getsockname reports the bound
endpoint (or 0.0.0.0:0 if unbound); getpeername short-circuits
with -ENOTCONN since we don't support connected UDP. For TCP:
both flavours ask the inet server via INET_CMD_TCP_GETNAME and
relay its status byte. Unallocated TCP sockets (no inet-side
slot yet — e.g. right after socket(SOCK_STREAM) before
connect/listen) report `0.0.0.0:0` for getsockname and
-ENOTCONN for getpeername.

Shared helper that drives the actual IPC + sockaddr formatting;
the two entry points differ only in "which" flag and in UDP's
peer short-circuit.

    shim_write_sockaddr(addr_ptr: i64, port: int, ip_octets: *byte)
        if addr_ptr == i64(0)
            return
        val a: *byte = *byte(addr_ptr)
        a[0] = byte(POSIX_AF_INET & 0xff)
        a[1] = byte(0)
        a[2] = byte(port >> 8)
        a[3] = byte(port & 0xff)
        a[4] = ip_octets[0]
        a[5] = ip_octets[1]
        a[6] = ip_octets[2]
        a[7] = ip_octets[3]
        var z = 8
        while z < 16
            a[z] = byte(0)
            z += 1

    shim_write_addrlen(addrlen_ptr: i64, n: int)
        if addrlen_ptr == i64(0)
            return
        val alp: *i32 = *i32(addrlen_ptr)
        *alp = i32(n)

    sys_getsockname(fd: int, addr_ptr: i64, addrlen_ptr: i64) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            val inet_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
            if inet_fd < 0
                return i64(-9)
            val port = inet_get_port()
            if port < 0
                return i64(-19)
            var msg: [2]byte
            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_UDP_GETNAME)
            msg[1] = byte(inet_fd & 0xff)
            var reply: [7]byte
            val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 2, reply, 7)
            if r < 0
                return i64(-5)
            if reply[0] != 0
                return i64(-5)
            val p = ((int(reply[1]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(reply[2]) & 0xff)
            shim_write_sockaddr(addr_ptr, p, &reply[3])
            shim_write_addrlen(addrlen_ptr, 16)
            return i64(0)
        if k == POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            val tcp_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
            if tcp_fd < 0
                // Unallocated TCP socket → 0.0.0.0:0 for getsockname.
                var zero: [4]byte
                zero[0] = byte(0)
                zero[1] = byte(0)
                zero[2] = byte(0)
                zero[3] = byte(0)
                shim_write_sockaddr(addr_ptr, 0, &zero[0])
                shim_write_addrlen(addrlen_ptr, 16)
                return i64(0)
            val port = inet_get_port()
            if port < 0
                return i64(-19)
            var msg: [4]byte
            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_GETNAME)
            shim_write_u16(msg, 1, tcp_fd)
            msg[3] = byte(0)               // which=0 → local
            var reply: [7]byte
            val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 4, reply, 7)
            if r < 0
                return i64(-5)
            val raw = int(reply[0]) & 0xff
            if raw != 0
                if raw >= 128
                    return i64(raw - 256)
                return i64(raw)
            val p = ((int(reply[1]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(reply[2]) & 0xff)
            shim_write_sockaddr(addr_ptr, p, &reply[3])
            shim_write_addrlen(addrlen_ptr, 16)
            return i64(0)
        i64(-9)                             // -EBADF

    sys_getpeername(fd: int, addr_ptr: i64, addrlen_ptr: i64) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            return i64(-107)               // -ENOTCONN (no connected UDP)
        if k != POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            return i64(-9)
        val tcp_fd = posix_fd_target(fd)
        if tcp_fd < 0
            return i64(-107)
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-19)
        var msg: [4]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_TCP_GETNAME)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 1, tcp_fd)
        msg[3] = byte(1)                   // which=1 → peer
        var reply: [7]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 4, reply, 7)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-5)
        val status = i8(reply[0])
        if int(status) != 0
            return i64(int(status))
        val p = ((int(reply[1]) & 0xff) << 8) | (int(reply[2]) & 0xff)
        shim_write_sockaddr(addr_ptr, p, &reply[3])
        shim_write_addrlen(addrlen_ptr, 16)
        i64(0)

SYS_DUP / SYS_DUP3. POSIX fd aliasing for the socket fd table.
The new fd shares `kind`, `target`, and `flags` with the old —
the inet record (or stdio kind) lives across both. Real refcount
is implicit: `sys_close` scans the rest of the table and skips
the inet close if any other slot still references the same
target. CLOEXEC (`flags & 1` in the dup3 call) is accepted but
not tracked, since SLIX has no `exec` yet.

`dup` allocates the lowest free fd via the standard table walk;
`dup3` writes the explicit `newfd`, closing whatever was there
first (also dup-aware via `sys_close`). dup3 with `oldfd ==
newfd` reports -EINVAL per Linux.

    sys_dup(oldfd: int) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(oldfd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_FREE
            return i64(-9)              // -EBADF
        val target = posix_fd_target(oldfd)
        val flags = posix_fd_flags(oldfd)
        val newfd = posix_fd_alloc(k, target)
        if newfd < 0
            return i64(-24)             // -EMFILE
        posix_fd_set_flags(newfd, flags)
        i64(newfd)

    sys_dup3(oldfd: int, newfd: int, dup_flags: int) -> i64
        if oldfd == newfd
            return i64(-22)             // -EINVAL
        val k = posix_fd_kind(oldfd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_FREE
            return i64(-9)
        if newfd < 0
            return i64(-9)
        if newfd >= POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC
            return i64(-9)
        if posix_fd_kind(newfd) != POSIX_FD_FREE
            val _ = sys_close(newfd)
        val target = posix_fd_target(oldfd)
        val ofl = posix_fd_flags(oldfd)
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val idx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + newfd
        posix_fd_table[idx].kind = k
        posix_fd_table[idx].target = target
        posix_fd_table[idx].flags = ofl
        posix_fd_table[idx].pos = i64(0)
        i64(newfd)

SYS_FCNTL. MVP dispatch for the handful of cmd codes musl programs
actually exercise before doing I/O:

- `F_GETFL` (3): return the slot's stored flags. Only O_NONBLOCK is
  meaningful today; other bits echo back whatever was set so
  `getfl | O_NONBLOCK` round-trips cleanly.
- `F_SETFL` (4): store `arg & O_NONBLOCK` in the slot. We mask so
  the shim can't be tricked into remembering unsupported flags and
  acting surprisingly later.
- `F_GETFD` (1) / `F_SETFD` (2): file-descriptor flags (mainly
  FD_CLOEXEC). SLIX has no `exec` yet, so CLOEXEC is a no-op.
  GETFD returns 0; SETFD accepts any value and returns 0. The
  day an exec path lands, this is the place to start tracking.
- `F_DUPFD` (0): not implemented — socket dup requires a separate
  fd-aliasing design (see POSIX-fd-bridge memory). Returns -EINVAL.

Unknown command → -EINVAL so real misuse surfaces.

    sys_fcntl(fd: int, cmd: int, arg: int) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_FREE
            return i64(-9)              // -EBADF
        if cmd == POSIX_F_GETFL
            return i64(posix_fd_flags(fd))
        if cmd == POSIX_F_SETFL
            posix_fd_set_flags(fd, arg & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK)
            return i64(0)
        if cmd == POSIX_F_GETFD
            return i64(0)
        if cmd == POSIX_F_SETFD
            return i64(0)
        if cmd == POSIX_F_DUPFD
            return i64(-22)             // -EINVAL (no dup yet)
        i64(-22)

SYS_OPENAT. The user passes `(dirfd, pathname, flags, mode)`. We
ignore `dirfd` (musl always sends `AT_FDCWD = -100` once the cwd
arc lands; for now the path must be absolute) and `mode` (file
creation isn't routed yet). The path is null-terminated in user
memory; we copy it into a stack buffer and forward to VFS as
`VFS_CMD_OPEN_C` (path-based, file scheme). Scheme-prefixed
paths ("tty:0", "tcp:host:port") would need `VFS_CMD_CONNECT_C`
instead, but that path requires VFS to route by command byte
which it doesn't today — adding it is a follow-up if we need
TTY/socket-via-open. The returned VFS handle is parked in a
fresh `POSIX_FD_FILE` slot.

    sys_openat(dirfd: int, path_ptr: i64, oflags: int, mode: int) -> i64
        if path_ptr == i64(0)
            return i64(-14)             // -EFAULT
        val port = vfs_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-19)             // -ENODEV
        var path: [128]byte
        val up: *byte = *byte(path_ptr)
        var n = 0
        while n < 127
            val c = up[n]
            if c == byte(0)
                break
            path[n] = c
            n += 1
        var msg: [129]byte
        msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_OPEN_C)
        var i = 0
        while i < n
            msg[1 + i] = path[i]
            i += 1
        var reply: [9]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 1 + n, reply, 9)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-5)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-2)              // -ENOENT
        val handle = shim_read_be_i32(reply, 1)
        val pfd = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_FILE, handle)
        if pfd < 0
            // Couldn't park the handle — VFS already counted it,
            // so close the backend before reporting EMFILE.
            var cmsg: [5]byte
            cmsg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_CLOSE_C)
            shim_write_be_i32(cmsg, 1, handle)
            var crep: [1]byte
            ipc_send(port, cmsg, 5, crep, 1)
            return i64(-24)             // -EMFILE
        posix_fd_set_flags(pfd, oflags & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK)
        i64(pfd)

SYS_LSEEK. VFS tracks file position server-side, so we just
forward `whence` (0=SEEK_SET, 1=SEEK_CUR; SEEK_END not yet
supported by VFS) and the offset. VFS replies with the new
absolute position. Non-file kinds (sockets, STDIO) report ESPIPE
the way Linux does for unseekable fds.

    sys_lseek(fd: int, offset: i64, whence: int) -> i64
        val k = posix_fd_kind(fd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_FREE
            return i64(-9)              // -EBADF
        if k != POSIX_FD_FILE
            return i64(-29)             // -ESPIPE
        val handle = posix_fd_target(fd)
        val port = vfs_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-19)
        var msg: [10]byte
        msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_SEEK_C)
        shim_write_be_i32(msg, 1, handle)
        shim_write_be_i32(msg, 5, int(offset))
        msg[9] = byte(whence)
        var reply: [5]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 10, reply, 5)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-5)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-22)             // -EINVAL
        i64(shim_read_be_i32(reply, 1))

SYS_PIPE2. Asks VFS to allocate a pipe and hand back two
handles; we install both in fresh `POSIX_FD_FILE` slots and
write the pair into `pipefd_array[0..1]` in user memory. Flags
(`O_NONBLOCK` / `O_CLOEXEC`) are stored on each slot — non-block
takes effect on subsequent reads, CLOEXEC is a placeholder until
exec lands.

    sys_pipe2(pipefd_array: i64, pipe_flags: int) -> i64
        if pipefd_array == i64(0)
            return i64(-14)
        val port = vfs_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return i64(-19)
        var msg: [1]byte
        msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_PIPE_C)
        var reply: [9]byte
        val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 1, reply, 9)
        if r < 0
            return i64(-5)
        if reply[0] != 0
            return i64(-24)             // -EMFILE
        val rh = shim_read_be_i32(reply, 1)
        val wh = shim_read_be_i32(reply, 5)
        val rfd = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_FILE, rh)
        val wfd = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_FILE, wh)
        if rfd < 0
            // Couldn't park the handles — close the backends so
            // VFS doesn't leak the pipe.
            var c1: [5]byte
            c1[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_CLOSE_C)
            shim_write_be_i32(c1, 1, rh)
            var crep: [1]byte
            ipc_send(port, c1, 5, crep, 1)
            shim_write_be_i32(c1, 1, wh)
            ipc_send(port, c1, 5, crep, 1)
            if wfd >= 0
                posix_fd_clear(wfd)
            return i64(-24)
        if wfd < 0
            sys_close(rfd)
            var c1: [5]byte
            c1[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_CLOSE_C)
            shim_write_be_i32(c1, 1, wh)
            var crep: [1]byte
            ipc_send(port, c1, 5, crep, 1)
            return i64(-24)
        val nb = pipe_flags & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
        if nb != 0
            posix_fd_set_flags(rfd, nb)
            posix_fd_set_flags(wfd, nb)
        var fdp: *int = *int(pipefd_array)
        fdp[0] = rfd
        fdp[1] = wfd
        i64(0)

### Epoll → inet subscription helpers

`epoll_inet_kind_for_fd` returns the inet-side `kind` byte
(1=UDP, 2=TCP) for a watched POSIX fd, or 0 if it's not a
socket — files / pipes / stdio don't talk to inet, so the SUB
roundtrip is skipped for them and they fall back to the
always-ready stopgap in `epoll_query_ready`.

    epoll_inet_kind_for_fd(target_fd: int) -> int
        val k = posix_fd_kind(target_fd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
            return 1
        if k == POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            return 2
        0

Send `INET_CMD_EPOLL_SUB` for a watched socket fd. Inet's
subscriber table is keyed on (sender_tid, kind, inet_fd,
epoll_inst); duplicates are no-ops so we don't have to
remember whether we've already subscribed. Errors are
swallowed — the worst case is that wake-on-edge degrades to
the polling fallback (which `sys_epoll_pwait` keeps doing on
timeouts anyway).

    epoll_inet_subscribe(target_fd: int, inst: int)
        val kind = epoll_inet_kind_for_fd(target_fd)
        if kind == 0
            return
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return
        var msg: [5]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_EPOLL_SUB)
        msg[1] = byte(kind)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 2, posix_fd_target(target_fd))
        msg[4] = byte(inst & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 5, reply, 1)

    epoll_inet_unsubscribe(target_fd: int, inst: int)
        val kind = epoll_inet_kind_for_fd(target_fd)
        if kind == 0
            return
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return
        var msg: [5]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_EPOLL_UNSUB)
        msg[1] = byte(kind)
        shim_write_u16(msg, 2, posix_fd_target(target_fd))
        msg[4] = byte(inst & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 5, reply, 1)

`epoll_inst_close_inet` is called when the epoll fd itself
closes — INST_CLOSE drops every subscriber inet was holding for
this (sender_tid, inst) pair in one IPC, instead of looping
through entries with UNSUB.

    epoll_inst_close_inet(inst: int)
        val port = inet_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return
        var msg: [2]byte
        msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_EPOLL_INST_CLOSE)
        msg[1] = byte(inst & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 2, reply, 1)


`epoll_vfs_subscribe` mirrors the inet helper for VFS-backed fds
(POSIX_FD_FILE — pipes, regular files, TTYs). VFS resolves the
shipped handle to its `oft_idx` and keys the subscription on
`(oft_idx, sender_tid, inst)` so two fds aliasing the same OFT
(e.g. dup'd pipe ends) share one wake.

The wire layout matches `INET_CMD_EPOLL_SUB` after the cmd byte:
  [cmd(1), handle_be(4), inst(1)]
which is `vfs_msg_write_i32`-format (big-endian) for the handle
to stay consistent with the rest of the VFS protocol.

    epoll_vfs_subscribe(target_fd: int, inst: int)
        if posix_fd_kind(target_fd) != POSIX_FD_FILE
            return
        val port = vfs_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return
        val handle = posix_fd_target(target_fd)
        var msg: [6]byte
        msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_EPOLL_SUB_C)
        msg[1] = byte((handle >> 24) & 0xff)
        msg[2] = byte((handle >> 16) & 0xff)
        msg[3] = byte((handle >> 8) & 0xff)
        msg[4] = byte(handle & 0xff)
        msg[5] = byte(inst & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 6, reply, 1)

    epoll_vfs_unsubscribe(target_fd: int, inst: int)
        if posix_fd_kind(target_fd) != POSIX_FD_FILE
            return
        val port = vfs_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return
        val handle = posix_fd_target(target_fd)
        var msg: [6]byte
        msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_EPOLL_UNSUB_C)
        msg[1] = byte((handle >> 24) & 0xff)
        msg[2] = byte((handle >> 16) & 0xff)
        msg[3] = byte((handle >> 8) & 0xff)
        msg[4] = byte(handle & 0xff)
        msg[5] = byte(inst & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 6, reply, 1)

    epoll_inst_close_vfs(inst: int)
        val port = vfs_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return
        var msg: [2]byte
        msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_EPOLL_INST_CLOSE_C)
        msg[1] = byte(inst & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 2, reply, 1)


`epoll_tty_subscribe` covers POSIX_FD_STDIN. The TTY server
keys subscriptions on (console, sender_tid, inst); we hardcode
console=0 since slix's per-process tty assignment is single-
console today.

    epoll_tty_subscribe(target_fd: int, inst: int)
        if posix_fd_kind(target_fd) != POSIX_FD_STDIN
            return
        val port = tty_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return
        var msg: [3]byte
        msg[0] = byte(TTY_CMD_EPOLL_SUB_C)
        msg[1] = byte(0)        // console
        msg[2] = byte(inst & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 3, reply, 1)

    epoll_tty_unsubscribe(target_fd: int, inst: int)
        if posix_fd_kind(target_fd) != POSIX_FD_STDIN
            return
        val port = tty_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return
        var msg: [3]byte
        msg[0] = byte(TTY_CMD_EPOLL_UNSUB_C)
        msg[1] = byte(0)
        msg[2] = byte(inst & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 3, reply, 1)

    epoll_inst_close_tty(inst: int)
        val port = tty_get_port()
        if port < 0
            return
        var msg: [2]byte
        msg[0] = byte(TTY_CMD_EPOLL_INST_CLOSE_C)
        msg[1] = byte(inst & 0xff)
        var reply: [1]byte
        ipc_send(port, msg, 2, reply, 1)


Combined sub/unsub helpers — single call site for the
`epoll_ctl` path, no need to teach it about the kind/owner
split. Each underlying helper short-circuits if the fd doesn't
belong to it, so calling both is safe and idempotent.

    epoll_subscribe(target_fd: int, inst: int)
        epoll_inet_subscribe(target_fd, inst)
        epoll_vfs_subscribe(target_fd, inst)
        epoll_tty_subscribe(target_fd, inst)

    epoll_unsubscribe(target_fd: int, inst: int)
        epoll_inet_unsubscribe(target_fd, inst)
        epoll_vfs_unsubscribe(target_fd, inst)
        epoll_tty_unsubscribe(target_fd, inst)

    epoll_inst_close_all(inst: int)
        epoll_inst_close_inet(inst)
        epoll_inst_close_vfs(inst)
        epoll_inst_close_tty(inst)


SYS_EPOLL_CREATE1. Allocates a fresh epoll instance and parks
it in a `POSIX_FD_EPOLL` slot. `flags` would carry
`EPOLL_CLOEXEC` / `EPOLL_NONBLOCK`; we honour O_NONBLOCK in
the slot but exec/CLOEXEC isn't tracked yet — see the
posix_fd_bridge memory.

SYS_EVENTFD2 (slix-musl syscall 156, sits next to the
epoll triplet at 153/154/155). Allocates an
eventfd backing slot with `initval` as the starting counter.
`flags` honors:

- `EFD_NONBLOCK` (0x800 = 2048) — same bit as `O_NONBLOCK`,
  recorded on the slot's flags so read/write pick the
  EAGAIN path on starvation.
- `EFD_CLOEXEC`  (0x80000 = 524288) — slix doesn't track CLOEXEC
  yet; accepted and ignored (matches the existing accept4 stance).
- `EFD_SEMAPHORE` (1) — read decrements the counter by 1 instead
  of returning the full count and zeroing it.

Returns the allocated POSIX fd, or -EMFILE if no slot is free.

    val POSIX_EFD_SEMAPHORE = 1
    val POSIX_EFD_CLOEXEC   = 524288
    val POSIX_EFD_NONBLOCK  = 2048

    sys_eventfd2(initval: int, flags: int) -> i64
        val pfd = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_EVENTFD, 0)
        if pfd < 0
            return i64(-24)
        val pid = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val idx = pid * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + pfd
        posix_fd_table[idx].eventfd_count = i64(initval)
        var sem = 0
        if (flags & POSIX_EFD_SEMAPHORE) != 0
            sem = 1
        posix_fd_table[idx].eventfd_semaphore = sem
        var fd_flags = 0
        if (flags & POSIX_EFD_NONBLOCK) != 0
            fd_flags = fd_flags | POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
        posix_fd_set_flags(pfd, fd_flags)
        i64(pfd)


SYS_TIMERFD_CREATE / SETTIME / GETTIME (slix-musl 356/357/358).
Periodic / one-shot kernel-managed timer fd. Read returns the
number of expirations since the last read; epoll EPOLLIN fires
when expirations > 0.

Slix runs the scheduler at 100 Hz, so sub-10 ms precision rounds
down to whole ticks. `CLOCK_REALTIME` and `CLOCK_MONOTONIC` are
both backed by `uptime()` here — slix has no wall-clock-vs-
monotonic distinction, so the two clockids resolve to the same
tick stream.  Programs that depend on a real wall clock (e.g.
DST handling) won't get one until a real RTC bridge lands.

Time conversion: 1 tick = 10 ms = 10_000_000 ns. We round down
on the nsec → tick conversion, which means a `tv_nsec=5_000_000`
(5 ms) request lands at "next whole tick" and may fire up to
10 ms late.  Programs needing finer precision lose nothing they
weren't going to lose to scheduler quanta anyway.

`flags` (TFD_NONBLOCK = 2048, TFD_CLOEXEC = 524288) — same bits
as O_NONBLOCK / O_CLOEXEC. Non-blocking is honoured; CLOEXEC
accept-and-ignore (matches accept4 / eventfd2).

`itimerspec` is a 32-byte LP64 struct: it_interval{tv_sec(8),
tv_nsec(8)} + it_value{tv_sec(8), tv_nsec(8)}.

    val POSIX_TFD_NONBLOCK  = 2048
    val POSIX_TFD_CLOEXEC   = 524288
    val POSIX_TFD_TIMER_ABSTIME = 1
    val POSIX_CLOCK_REALTIME  = 0
    val POSIX_CLOCK_MONOTONIC = 1

    shim_itimerspec_to_ticks(p: *byte, off: int) -> int
        // Read tv_sec (low 8B) + tv_nsec (high 8B). Slix's tick
        // resolution is 10 ms so we don't need the high half of
        // tv_sec for any sane timer; clamp to int range.
        var sec_lo = 0
        var bi = 0
        while bi < 4
            sec_lo = sec_lo | ((int(p[off + bi]) & 0xff) << (bi * 8))
            bi += 1
        var nsec_lo = 0
        bi = 0
        while bi < 4
            nsec_lo = nsec_lo | ((int(p[off + 8 + bi]) & 0xff) << (bi * 8))
            bi += 1
        if sec_lo < 0
            sec_lo = 0
        if nsec_lo < 0
            nsec_lo = 0
        sec_lo * 100 + (nsec_lo / 10000000)

    shim_ticks_to_itimerspec(p: *byte, off: int, ticks: int)
        // Write tv_sec (8B LE) + tv_nsec (8B LE). Use i64 for the
        // shift-and-mask loop so byte-extraction past bit 31 doesn't
        // hit the i32 shift-by-≥-width undefined-behaviour path some
        // sysl backends fold into a no-op.
        val sec = i64(ticks / 100)
        val ns = i64((ticks % 100) * 10000000)
        var bi = 0
        while bi < 8
            p[off + bi] = byte(int((sec >> i64(bi * 8))) & 0xff)
            bi += 1
        bi = 0
        while bi < 8
            p[off + 8 + bi] = byte(int((ns >> i64(bi * 8))) & 0xff)
            bi += 1


Catch up `expirations` to current uptime: every full `interval`
between `next_tick` and now becomes one expiration. Returns
without doing anything if the timer is disarmed or hasn't fired
yet. Called lazily from sys_read / epoll_query_ready so the timer
state is always coherent at the moment the user observes it.

    timerfd_advance(idx: int)
        if posix_fd_table[idx].timerfd_next_tick == 0
            return
        val now = int(uptime())
        if now < posix_fd_table[idx].timerfd_next_tick
            return
        val interval = posix_fd_table[idx].timerfd_interval_ticks
        if interval <= 0
            // One-shot timer: a single expiration, then disarm.
            val cur1 = posix_fd_table[idx].timerfd_expirations
            posix_fd_table[idx].timerfd_expirations = cur1 + i64(1)
            posix_fd_table[idx].timerfd_next_tick = 0
            return
        // Periodic: catch up by however many full intervals have
        // elapsed past `next_tick`.  +1 covers the boundary tick
        // itself.
        val past = now - posix_fd_table[idx].timerfd_next_tick
        val fired = (past / interval) + 1
        val cur2 = posix_fd_table[idx].timerfd_expirations
        posix_fd_table[idx].timerfd_expirations = cur2 + i64(fired)
        val nt = posix_fd_table[idx].timerfd_next_tick
        posix_fd_table[idx].timerfd_next_tick = nt + fired * interval


    sys_timerfd_create(clockid: int, flags: int) -> i64
        if clockid != POSIX_CLOCK_REALTIME
            if clockid != POSIX_CLOCK_MONOTONIC
                return i64(-22)
        val pfd = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_TIMERFD, 0)
        if pfd < 0
            return i64(-24)
        var fd_flags = 0
        if (flags & POSIX_TFD_NONBLOCK) != 0
            fd_flags = fd_flags | POSIX_O_NONBLOCK
        posix_fd_set_flags(pfd, fd_flags)
        i64(pfd)


    sys_timerfd_settime(fd: int, flags: int, new_value: i64, old_value: i64) -> i64
        if posix_fd_kind(fd) != POSIX_FD_TIMERFD
            return i64(-22)
        if new_value == i64(0)
            return i64(-14)
        val pid_t = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val tidx = pid_t * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
        // Catch up before snapshotting old_value so any
        // unobserved expirations are accounted to the previous
        // configuration.
        timerfd_advance(tidx)
        // Stash old value if requested (interval, then remaining
        // time-to-fire).
        if old_value != i64(0)
            val oldp: *byte = *byte(old_value)
            shim_ticks_to_itimerspec(oldp, 0,
                posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_interval_ticks)
            var remaining = 0
            if posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_next_tick > 0
                remaining = posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_next_tick - int(uptime())
                if remaining < 0
                    remaining = 0
            shim_ticks_to_itimerspec(oldp, 16, remaining)
        val newp: *byte = *byte(new_value)
        val it_interval_ticks = shim_itimerspec_to_ticks(newp, 0)
        val it_value_ticks = shim_itimerspec_to_ticks(newp, 16)
        // it_value == 0 disarms the timer (Linux semantics).
        if it_value_ticks <= 0
            posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_interval_ticks = 0
            posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_next_tick = 0
            return i64(0)
        // TFD_TIMER_ABSTIME: it_value is an absolute tick (treating
        // tv_sec*100+tv_nsec/1e7 as ticks-since-boot).  Otherwise
        // it_value is relative to now.  Slix collapses both clocks
        // to uptime() so the meaning of "absolute" matches whatever
        // the caller expects from CLOCK_MONOTONIC.
        if (flags & POSIX_TFD_TIMER_ABSTIME) != 0
            posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_next_tick = it_value_ticks
        else
            posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_next_tick = int(uptime()) + it_value_ticks
        posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_interval_ticks = it_interval_ticks
        // Reset expirations to 0 on rearm (Linux: setttime starts
        // fresh; the read counter is wiped).
        posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_expirations = i64(0)
        i64(0)


    sys_timerfd_gettime(fd: int, curr_value: i64) -> i64
        if posix_fd_kind(fd) != POSIX_FD_TIMERFD
            return i64(-22)
        if curr_value == i64(0)
            return i64(-14)
        val pid_t = posix_fd_current_pid()
        val tidx = pid_t * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + fd
        // Catch up so the snapshot reflects the live timer state.
        timerfd_advance(tidx)
        val cp: *byte = *byte(curr_value)
        shim_ticks_to_itimerspec(cp, 0,
            posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_interval_ticks)
        var remaining = 0
        if posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_next_tick > 0
            remaining = posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_next_tick - int(uptime())
            if remaining < 0
                remaining = 0
        shim_ticks_to_itimerspec(cp, 16, remaining)
        i64(0)


    sys_epoll_create1(epoll_flags: int) -> i64
        val inst = epoll_alloc_inst()
        if inst < 0
            return i64(-24)             // -EMFILE
        val pfd = posix_fd_alloc(POSIX_FD_EPOLL, inst)
        if pfd < 0
            epoll_inst_release(inst)
            return i64(-24)
        posix_fd_set_flags(pfd, epoll_flags & POSIX_O_NONBLOCK)
        i64(pfd)

`epoll_event` field readers. LP64 layout is `events: u32 @0`,
padding @4, `data: u64 @8`. We need byte-level reads because the
user pointer might not be aligned.

    shim_read_le_u32(p: *byte, off: int) -> int
        (int(p[off]) & 0xff) | ((int(p[off + 1]) & 0xff) << 8) | ((int(p[off + 2]) & 0xff) << 16) | ((int(p[off + 3]) & 0xff) << 24)

    shim_write_le_u32(p: *byte, off: int, v: int)
        p[off]     = byte(v & 0xff)
        p[off + 1] = byte((v >> 8) & 0xff)
        p[off + 2] = byte((v >> 16) & 0xff)
        p[off + 3] = byte((v >> 24) & 0xff)

    shim_read_le_i64(p: *byte, off: int) -> i64
        var v = i64(0)
        var i = 7
        while i >= 0
            v = (v << i64(8)) | i64(int(p[off + i]) & 0xff)
            i -= 1
        v

    shim_write_le_i64(p: *byte, off: int, v: i64)
        var i = 0
        while i < 8
            p[off + i] = byte(int((v >> i64(8 * i))) & 0xff)
            i += 1

SYS_EPOLL_CTL. ADD finds a free entry (and rejects if `fd` is
already registered, like Linux); MOD updates an existing
entry's events/data; DEL drops it. Returns 0 on success, errno
on failure. We don't validate that `fd` is one of the kinds we
can actually poll (sockets, pipes, files) because epoll_pwait
will just see "always not ready" for unknown kinds — same
end-user effect as Linux's "polled but never fires" for fds
without poll support.

    sys_epoll_ctl(epfd: int, op: int, target_fd: int, ev_ptr: i64) -> i64
        if posix_fd_kind(epfd) != POSIX_FD_EPOLL
            return i64(-22)             // -EINVAL
        val inst = posix_fd_target(epfd)
        if inst < 0
            return i64(-22)
        var events_in = 0
        var data_in = i64(0)
        if op != POSIX_EPOLL_CTL_DEL
            if ev_ptr == i64(0)
                return i64(-14)         // -EFAULT
            val up: *byte = *byte(ev_ptr)
            events_in = shim_read_le_u32(up, 0)
            data_in = shim_read_le_i64(up, 8)
        if op == POSIX_EPOLL_CTL_ADD
            // Reject duplicate fd registrations.
            var i = 0
            while i < POSIX_EPOLL_ENTRIES_PER_INSTANCE
                if epoll_table[inst].entries[i].in_use != 0
                    if epoll_table[inst].entries[i].watched_fd == target_fd
                        return i64(-17)     // -EEXIST
                i += 1
            i = 0
            while i < POSIX_EPOLL_ENTRIES_PER_INSTANCE
                if epoll_table[inst].entries[i].in_use == 0
                    epoll_table[inst].entries[i].in_use = 1
                    epoll_table[inst].entries[i].watched_fd = target_fd
                    epoll_table[inst].entries[i].events = events_in
                    epoll_table[inst].entries[i].data_lo = data_in
                    epoll_table[inst].entries[i].last_reported = 0
                    epoll_table[inst].entries[i].armed = 1
                    // Start at 0; the first POLL will sync against
                    // the server's current value. If the fd already
                    // had fires before ADD, the first scan sees seq
                    // advanced and (correctly) treats the current
                    // ready bits as a fresh edge.
                    epoll_table[inst].entries[i].last_seen_seq = 0
                    epoll_subscribe(target_fd, inst)
                    return i64(0)
                i += 1
            return i64(-28)             // -ENOSPC
        if op == POSIX_EPOLL_CTL_MOD
            var i = 0
            while i < POSIX_EPOLL_ENTRIES_PER_INSTANCE
                if epoll_table[inst].entries[i].in_use != 0
                    if epoll_table[inst].entries[i].watched_fd == target_fd
                        epoll_table[inst].entries[i].events = events_in
                        epoll_table[inst].entries[i].data_lo = data_in
                        // MOD re-arms a one-shot entry and resets the
                        // edge-trigger memory so the next ready check
                        // can fire even if the bits look the same as
                        // before — matches Linux's MOD-rearm semantics.
                        epoll_table[inst].entries[i].last_reported = 0
                        epoll_table[inst].entries[i].armed = 1
                        // Treat MOD as a fresh-edge boundary: forget
                        // the cached seq so the next POLL re-syncs.
                        epoll_table[inst].entries[i].last_seen_seq = 0
                        return i64(0)
                i += 1
            return i64(-2)              // -ENOENT
        if op == POSIX_EPOLL_CTL_DEL
            var i = 0
            while i < POSIX_EPOLL_ENTRIES_PER_INSTANCE
                if epoll_table[inst].entries[i].in_use != 0
                    if epoll_table[inst].entries[i].watched_fd == target_fd
                        epoll_unsubscribe(target_fd, inst)
                        epoll_table[inst].entries[i].in_use = 0
                        return i64(0)
                i += 1
            return i64(-2)
        i64(-22)

Query a single watched fd's readiness. Returns the bits the
shim should report, masked against the user's requested events
+ EPOLLERR/EPOLLHUP (which are always reported regardless of
what the user asked for, per Linux semantics). Also writes the
per-fd `fire_seq` (returned by both INET_CMD_POLL and
VFS_CMD_POLL) into `*seq_out` so `epoll_scan_once` can detect
"this specific fd actually fired since the last scan" without
the per-instance bulk last_reported clear that over-fires
multi-fd EPOLLET loops.

    epoll_query_ready(target_fd: int, want: int, seq_out: *int) -> int
        *seq_out = 0
        val k = posix_fd_kind(target_fd)
        if k == POSIX_FD_FREE
            return POSIX_EPOLLERR
        if k == POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET || k == POSIX_FD_TCP_SOCKET
            val port = inet_get_port()
            if port < 0
                return 0
            var msg: [4]byte
            msg[0] = byte(INET_CMD_POLL)
            if k == POSIX_FD_UDP_SOCKET
                msg[1] = byte(1)
            else
                msg[1] = byte(2)
            shim_write_u16(msg, 2, posix_fd_target(target_fd))
            var reply: [7]byte
            val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 4, reply, 7)
            if r < 0
                return POSIX_EPOLLERR
            if reply[0] != 0
                return POSIX_EPOLLERR
            val raw = shim_read_u16(reply, 1)
            *seq_out = (int(reply[3]) & 0xff) | ((int(reply[4]) & 0xff) << 8) | ((int(reply[5]) & 0xff) << 16) | ((int(reply[6]) & 0xff) << 24)
            // Mask: caller's requested events plus error/hangup
            // which are always reported.
            return raw & (want | POSIX_EPOLLERR | POSIX_EPOLLHUP)
        if k == POSIX_FD_FILE
            // VFS_CMD_POLL: shim ships the handle, vfs reports the
            // OFT-derived bits (regular files always-ready,
            // pipes per buffer count + peer-end state, TTYs
            // output-only).
            val port = vfs_get_port()
            if port < 0
                return 0
            val handle = posix_fd_target(target_fd)
            var msg: [5]byte
            msg[0] = byte(VFS_CMD_POLL_C)
            msg[1] = byte((handle >> 24) & 0xff)
            msg[2] = byte((handle >> 16) & 0xff)
            msg[3] = byte((handle >> 8) & 0xff)
            msg[4] = byte(handle & 0xff)
            var reply: [7]byte
            val r = ipc_send(port, msg, 5, reply, 7)
            if r < 0
                return POSIX_EPOLLERR
            if reply[0] != 0
                return POSIX_EPOLLERR
            val raw = (int(reply[1]) & 0xff) | ((int(reply[2]) & 0xff) << 8)
            *seq_out = (int(reply[3]) & 0xff) | ((int(reply[4]) & 0xff) << 8) | ((int(reply[5]) & 0xff) << 16) | ((int(reply[6]) & 0xff) << 24)
            return raw & (want | POSIX_EPOLLERR | POSIX_EPOLLHUP)
        if k == POSIX_FD_STDIN
            // Ask the tty server for readiness on console 0 plus
            // its per-console fire_seq. Reply mirrors VFS_CMD_POLL
            // so the existing 7-byte parse layout is reused.
            val port_t = tty_get_port()
            if port_t < 0
                return 0
            var msg: [2]byte
            msg[0] = byte(TTY_CMD_POLL_C)
            msg[1] = byte(0)
            var reply: [7]byte
            val r = ipc_send(port_t, msg, 2, reply, 7)
            if r < 0
                return 0
            if reply[0] != 0
                return 0
            val raw = (int(reply[1]) & 0xff) | ((int(reply[2]) & 0xff) << 8)
            *seq_out = (int(reply[3]) & 0xff) | ((int(reply[4]) & 0xff) << 8) | ((int(reply[5]) & 0xff) << 16) | ((int(reply[6]) & 0xff) << 24)
            return raw & (want | POSIX_EPOLLERR | POSIX_EPOLLHUP)
        if k == POSIX_FD_STDOUT || k == POSIX_FD_STDERR
            return want & POSIX_EPOLLOUT
        if k == POSIX_FD_EVENTFD
            // Readable when count > 0; writable when count <
            // u64-1 (i.e. add of 1 wouldn't overflow). Counter is
            // i64 internally with i64::MAX representing "near max"
            // — a tighter bound than Linux's u64::MAX-1, but the
            // gap is irrelevant for any practical use.
            val pid_e = posix_fd_current_pid()
            val eidx = pid_e * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + target_fd
            val cnt = posix_fd_table[eidx].eventfd_count
            var ev = 0
            if cnt > i64(0)
                ev = ev | POSIX_EPOLLIN
            if cnt < i64(0x7fffffffffffffff)
                ev = ev | POSIX_EPOLLOUT
            return ev & (want | POSIX_EPOLLERR | POSIX_EPOLLHUP)
        if k == POSIX_FD_TIMERFD
            // Catch up before reporting so a fired-but-unread
            // timer registers as readable on the entry-pass scan.
            val pid_t = posix_fd_current_pid()
            val tidx = pid_t * POSIX_FDS_PER_PROC + target_fd
            timerfd_advance(tidx)
            var ev = 0
            if posix_fd_table[tidx].timerfd_expirations > i64(0)
                ev = ev | POSIX_EPOLLIN
            return ev & (want | POSIX_EPOLLERR | POSIX_EPOLLHUP)
        0

`epoll_scan_once` walks the instance's entries, queries inet
once per active entry, and writes ready events into
`events_array`. EPOLLET filters down to the rising edges since
the last report (`last_reported`); EPOLLONESHOT disarms the
entry after firing once (re-armed by `EPOLL_CTL_MOD`). Returns
the number of events written.

    epoll_scan_once(inst: int, out: *byte, max_events: int) -> int
        var produced = 0
        var i = 0
        while i < POSIX_EPOLL_ENTRIES_PER_INSTANCE
            if produced >= max_events
                break
            if epoll_table[inst].entries[i].in_use != 0
                if epoll_table[inst].entries[i].armed != 0
                    val tfd = epoll_table[inst].entries[i].watched_fd
                    val want = epoll_table[inst].entries[i].events
                    var seq = 0
                    val ready = epoll_query_ready(tfd, want, &seq)
                    // Per-fd edge detection: when the server-side
                    // counter advances past our cached value, the
                    // fd actually fired since the last scan. Reset
                    // `last_reported` so EPOLLET sees the live bits
                    // as rising again — without clobbering siblings
                    // in the same epoll instance whose counters
                    // didn't move.
                    if seq != epoll_table[inst].entries[i].last_seen_seq
                        epoll_table[inst].entries[i].last_reported = 0
                        epoll_table[inst].entries[i].last_seen_seq = seq
                    var deliver = ready
                    if (want & POSIX_EPOLLET) != 0
                        // Only the bits that just became ready since
                        // the last report. If the user fully drained
                        // the fd they'd have called recv until -EAGAIN
                        // (we don't simulate that — readiness is
                        // computed each time from the live socket
                        // state), so any rising edge fires once.
                        val rising = ready & ~epoll_table[inst].entries[i].last_reported
                        deliver = rising
                    epoll_table[inst].entries[i].last_reported = ready
                    if deliver != 0
                        val off = produced * POSIX_EPOLL_EVENT_SIZE
                        shim_write_le_u32(out, off, deliver)
                        shim_write_le_i64(out, off + 8,
                            epoll_table[inst].entries[i].data_lo)
                        produced += 1
                        if (want & POSIX_EPOLLONESHOT) != 0
                            // Disarm — entry stays in_use=1 so a
                            // subsequent EPOLL_CTL_MOD can re-arm it,
                            // but no further fires until then.
                            epoll_table[inst].entries[i].armed = 0
            i += 1
        produced

SYS_EPOLL_PWAIT. Phase A2: blocks via the kernel notify
primitive (or `sleep_or_notify` for finite timeouts) instead of
the old sleep-1-tick loop. inet and VFS each maintain a per-fd
subscriber list that fires `notify_send_to(tid, 1)` on every
readiness boundary, so a wait wakes within a syscall hop of the
change. `sigmask` is ignored (no signal delivery yet).

The earlier per-instance `last_reported` bulk clear has been
replaced with per-entry seq comparison inside `epoll_scan_once`:
each POLL reply carries the server's monotonic per-fd fire
counter, and only entries whose counter advanced since our last
scan get their `last_reported` reset. `notify_read_self()` is
still drained here so the kernel's notify_pending gauge clears
and `notify_wait()` can block again on the next iteration.

    sys_epoll_pwait(epfd: int, events_array: i64, max_events: int, timeout_ms: int) -> i64
        if posix_fd_kind(epfd) != POSIX_FD_EPOLL
            return i64(-22)
        val inst = posix_fd_target(epfd)
        if inst < 0
            return i64(-22)
        if max_events <= 0
            return i64(-22)
        if events_array == i64(0)
            return i64(-14)
        val out: *byte = *byte(events_array)
        var ticks_left = -1
        if timeout_ms >= 0
            ticks_left = (timeout_ms + 9) / 10
        var iter = 0
        while iter < 100000             // runaway-loop guard
            iter += 1
            // Drain any pending notify so notify_wait() can block
            // cleanly on the next loop iteration. The seq-based
            // edge detection inside epoll_scan_once handles the
            // EPOLLET clearing now — no per-instance bulk reset.
            val _ = notify_read_self()
            val produced = epoll_scan_once(inst, out, max_events)
            if produced > 0
                return i64(produced)
            if timeout_ms == 0
                return i64(0)
            if ticks_left == 0
                return i64(0)
            if timeout_ms < 0
                // Block until inet/VFS (or someone) wakes us. Spurious
                // wakes loop back to the scan above — no-op cost.
                notify_wait()
            else
                // sleep_or_notify(ticks_left) wakes on either deadline
                // or notify. Spurious wakes lose at most one extra
                // pass; we record uptime() before/after to charge the
                // actual elapsed ticks to ticks_left so a chain of
                // spurious wakes still respects the deadline.
                val before = uptime()
                sleep_or_notify(ticks_left)
                val after = uptime()
                val elapsed = after - before
                if elapsed >= ticks_left
                    ticks_left = 0
                else
                    ticks_left -= elapsed
        i64(0)
